OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026", "title": "Almost-everywhere Fourier convergence in L log L", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "075", "family_title": "The \\(L\\log L\\) Fourier-convergence conjecture", "abstract_html": "We prove that the ordinary symmetric Fourier partial sums of every complex-valued function in \\(L\\log L\\) on the circle converge almost everywhere to the function along the full sequence. This establishes the classical \\(L\\log L\\) sufficiency conjecture.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Almost-everywhere convergence in \\(L\\log L\\) — original Theorem 1.1.** Let \\(\\mathbb T=\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)\\), with normalized Lebesgue measure \\(d\\mu(x)=dx/(2\\pi)\\).", "statement": "<p>**Almost-everywhere convergence in \\(L\\log L\\) — original Theorem 1.1.**</p><p>Let \\(\\mathbb T=\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)\\), with normalized Lebesgue measure \\(d\\mu(x)=dx/(2\\pi)\\). For an integrable complex-valued function \\(f\\), define\n\\[\n\\widehat f(k)=\\int_{\\mathbb T}f(x)e^{-ikx}\\,d\\mu(x),\n\\qquad\nS_Nf(x)=\\sum_{k=-N}^{N}\\widehat f(k)e^{ikx}.\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\nL\\log L(\\mathbb T)\n=\n\\left\\{f:\\int_{\\mathbb T}|f|\\log(2+|f|)\\,d\\mu&lt;\\infty\\right\\}.\n\\]</p><p>For every complex-valued \\(f\\in L\\log L(\\mathbb T)\\), there is a measurable null set \\(N_f\\) such that\n\\[\n\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}S_Nf(x)=f(x)\n\\qquad(x\\in\\mathbb T\\setminus N_f).\n\\]\nThe limit is along the full sequence \\(N=0,1,2,\\ldots\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the height-layer summation of original Section 10.3.</p><p>Work first in unit-period coordinates. Write\n\\[\nf=f_{\\mathrm{bd}}+\\sum_{j\\ge1}f_j,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nf_{\\mathrm{bd}}=f\\mathbf1_{\\{|f|&lt;e^2\\}},\n\\qquad\nf_j=f\\mathbf1_{\\{e^{2^j}\\le|f|&lt;e^{2^{j+1}}\\}}.\n\\]\nSplit each \\(f_j\\) into four nonnegative parts with coefficients \\(1,-1,i,-i\\). Consider one component sequence \\(q_j\\), discard zero masses, and set\n\\[\nm_j=\\int q_j,\\qquad\n\\rho_j=q_j/m_j,\\qquad\nd_j=2^{j+1}+\\log^-m_j.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\int\\rho_j=1\\) and \\(\\rho_j\\le e^{d_j}\\).</p><p>We have\n\\[\n\\sum_jm_jd_j&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nIndeed, disjointness of the layers and \\(\\log|f|\\ge2^j\\) on their supports give\n\\[\n\\sum_j2^{j+1}m_j\n\\le2\\int_{\\{|f|\\ge e^2\\}}|f|\\log|f|&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nIf \\(m_j\\ge e^{-2^j}\\), then\n\\[\nm_j\\log^-m_j\\le2^jm_j.\n\\]\nIf \\(m_j&lt;e^{-2^j}\\), monotonicity of \\(t\\log(1/t)\\) on \\((0,e^{-1})\\) gives\n\\[\nm_j\\log(1/m_j)\\le2^je^{-2^j}.\n\\]\nBoth bounds are summable.</p><p>For all sufficiently large \\(j\\), apply Proposition 9 to \\(\\rho_j\\), obtaining \\(G_j\\). Since\n\\[\n\\log d_j\\ge(j+1)\\log2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_j|G_j^c|\n\\le C\\sum_j(\\log d_j)^{-4}&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nBorel–Cantelli shows that almost every point belongs to all but finitely many \\(G_j\\).</p><p>Tonelli and Proposition 9 give\n\\[\n\\int\\sum_j\\mathbf1_{G_j}(x)\\sup_N|S_Nq_j(x)|\\,dx\n\\le C\\sum_jm_jd_j&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nThus the partial-sum maxima of tail layers are absolutely summable almost everywhere. Each individual \\(q_j\\) is bounded, so Proposition 10 gives its full-sequence limit and finite partial-sum maximum outside a null set. Restore the finitely many initially omitted layers. Intersect these full-measure sets for the four component sequences and \\(f_{\\mathrm{bd}}\\).</p><p>The layers sum in \\(L^1\\). For each fixed \\(N\\), Fourier coefficients can therefore be summed term by term; equivalently, the finite-rank \\(S_N\\) is bounded from \\(L^1\\) to \\(L^\\infty\\). Hence\n\\[\nS_Nf=S_Nf_{\\mathrm{bd}}+\\sum_{j\\ge1}S_Nf_j.\n\\]\nAt every point of the chosen full-measure set, the absolute sum of component partial-sum maxima is finite. Its tail tends to zero uniformly in \\(N\\). First take the full-sequence limit on finitely many layers using Proposition 10, and then increase the finite collection. This gives\n\\[\nS_Nf(x)\\to f_{\\mathrm{bd}}(x)+\\sum_jf_j(x)=f(x).\n\\]\nOnly countably many measurable null sets were removed. Returning to \\(x=2\\pi y\\) gives the stated normalization and conclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Weak-\\(L^1\\) maximal bound — original Corollary 10.3.** On \\(\\mathbb T=\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)\\), with \\(d\\mu=dx/(2\\pi)\\), put …", "statement": "<p>**Weak-\\(L^1\\) maximal bound — original Corollary 10.3.**</p><p>On \\(\\mathbb T=\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)\\), with \\(d\\mu=dx/(2\\pi)\\), put\n\\[\n\\Phi(t)=t\\log(2+t),\n\\qquad\n\\|f\\|_\\Phi\n=\n\\inf\\left\\{b&gt;0:\\int_{\\mathbb T}\\Phi(|f|/b)\\,d\\mu\\le1\\right\\}.\n\\]\nFor the ordinary symmetric Fourier partial sums, define\n\\[\nS^*f=\\sup_{N\\ge0}|S_Nf|.\n\\]</p><p>There is an absolute constant \\(C\\) such that\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\lambda&gt;0}\\lambda\\,\\mu\\{S^*f&gt;\\lambda\\}\n\\le C\\|f\\|_\\Phi\n\\qquad\n\\bigl(f\\in L\\log L(\\mathbb T;\\mathbb C)\\bigr).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The real Luxemburg space\n\\[\nB=L^\\Phi(\\mathbb T;\\mathbb R)\n\\]\nis Banach. Since \\(\\Phi(2t)\\le4\\Phi(t)\\), its underlying set is exactly the real \\(L\\log L\\) class in Theorem 1. Also\n\\[\n\\|f\\|_1\\le\\|f\\|_\\Phi/\\log2.\n\\]\nTranslations are isometries of \\(B\\).</p><p>Every \\(S_N\\) is real linear, commutes with translations, and is bounded from \\(B\\) to itself, because\n\\[\n\\|S_Nf\\|_\\infty\\le(2N+1)\\|f\\|_1\n\\]\nand \\(L^\\infty\\) embeds continuously in \\(B\\). These bounds need not be uniform in \\(N\\). Theorem 1 gives \\(S^*f&lt;\\infty\\) almost everywhere for every real \\(f\\in B\\).</p><p>Apply Stein's Banach-space theorem cited in the paper as reference [22, Appendix, Theorem 10] to\n\\[\nS_0,-S_0,S_1,-S_1,\\ldots.\n\\]\nIts signed supremum is exactly \\(S^*f\\), so\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\lambda&gt;0}\\lambda\\,\\mu\\{S^*f&gt;\\lambda\\}\\le C\\|f\\|_\\Phi\n\\]\nfor real inputs.</p><p>For \\(f=u+iv\\),\n\\[\nS^*f\\le S^*u+S^*v,\n\\qquad\n\\|u\\|_\\Phi,\\|v\\|_\\Phi\\le\\|f\\|_\\Phi.\n\\]\nSplit the level at \\(\\lambda/2\\) and apply the real bound to both parts. Enlarging the absolute constant proves the complex-valued assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Entropy compression — original Lemma 3.1.** All logarithms are natural.", "statement": "<p>**Entropy compression — original Lemma 3.1.**</p><p>All logarithms are natural. Throughout this proposition,\n\\[\nh=\\log\\log d,\\quad\nT=\\lfloor dh^{-5}\\rfloor,\\quad\nw=(\\log d)^{1/4},\\quad\nL=d\\exp(w),\\quad\nm_*=\\lfloor\\exp(w^3)\\rfloor,\n\\]\nand \\(d\\) is sufficiently large. Write\n\\[\n\\chi_v(\\theta)=e^{2\\pi iv\\theta}.\n\\]\nFor a finite set \\(S\\subset\\mathbb Z\\), \\(nS\\) is its \\(n\\)-fold sumset with repetitions.</p><p>Fix \\(D_0,P_0\\ge1\\). Let \\(q\\) be a strictly positive probability mass function on \\(\\mathbb Z\\). Suppose that, for a positive integer \\(g\\) and a probability measure \\(\\omega\\) on \\(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\),\n\\[\nH_q(x):=\\frac{q(x)}{q(0)}\n=\\int\\chi_{gx}(\\theta)\\,d\\omega(\\theta),\n\\qquad\nF_0:=-\\log q(0)\\le D_0d.\n\\]\nLet \\(S\\subset\\mathbb Z\\) be finite and nonempty, and suppose\n\\[\n\\log|nS|\\le D_0dh^{P_0}\\log d\n\\qquad(1\\le n\\le d^2).\n\\]\nSuppose\n\\[\nd/h^2\\le E\\le D_0d\n\\]\nand, for every probability measure \\(\\lambda\\) on \\(S\\),\n\\[\n-\\log\\mathbb E_\\lambda\nH_q(X_1+\\cdots+X_T-X'_1-\\cdots-X'_T)\n\\le E,\n\\]\nwhere all \\(2T\\) letters are independent with law \\(\\lambda\\).</p><p>Then there are \\(x_0\\in S\\) and a nonempty \\(S'\\subset S\\) such that\n\\[\n\\log|S|-\\log|S'|\n\\le C(D_0)\\frac{d^2}{Eh^2},\n\\]\nand, for every probability measure \\(v\\) on this same \\(S'\\),\n\\[\n\\int\n\\exp\\left(\n-L\\sum_{x\\in S'}v(x)\n\\bigl(1-\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_{g(x-x_0)}(\\theta)\\bigr)\n\\right)\\,d\\omega(\\theta)\n\\ge e^{-CE}.\n\\]\nThe exponential constant is numerical. The sufficiently large threshold for \\(d\\) may depend on \\(D_0,P_0\\).</p><p>In the proof, \\(H\\), \\(D\\), and \\(I\\) denote Shannon entropy, relative entropy, and mutual information. Total variation is\n\\[\n\\|P-Q\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}=\\sup_A|P(A)-Q(A)|.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nc(u)=-\\log H_q(u).\n\\]\nThe characteristic-function hypothesis implies \\(0&lt;H_q(u)\\le1\\), so \\(c\\) is finite and nonnegative, with \\(c(0)=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Lemma 1.8 and put\n\\[\nm=m_{j,1},\\qquad r=r_j,\\qquad s=mr\\ge L.\n\\]\nLemmas 1.6 and 1.8 give\n\\[\nK_m(W;r)\\le CE.\n\\]\nLift a minimizing coupling to all \\(s\\) letters by Lemma 1.5. Write \\(\\mathbf W=(W_i)_{i=1}^s\\), and let \\(\\nu_z=\\mathcal L(X\\mid W=z)\\).</p><p>For a positive-probability stack \\(\\mathbf w\\), define, under its actual conditional law,\n\\[\nC(\\mathbf w)\n=\nD\\left(X^s\\,\\middle\\|\\,\\prod_i\\nu_{w_i}\\right)\n+I(X^s;Z)+\\mathbb Ec(\\Gamma),\n\\qquad\nZ=\\sum_iX_i+\\Gamma.\n\\]\nThe comparator has no zeros on events of positive conditional probability. The prescribed row marginals give\n\\[\n\\mathbb EC(\\mathbf W)=K_m(W;r)\\le CE.\n\\]\nAlso, for\n\\[\nB(\\mathbf w)=s^{-1}\\sum_iD(\\nu_{w_i}\\|U_S),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbb EB(\\mathbf W)\n=\n\\log|S|-H(X\\mid W)\n\\le CE/T+\\frac{CD_*^2}{Eh^2}\n\\le\\frac{C(D_0)d^2}{Eh^2}.\n\\]\nThe \\(E/T\\) term is absorbed because \\(T\\asymp d/h^5\\), \\(E\\le D_0d\\), and \\(E^2h^7/d^3\\to0\\). Markov's inequality selects one stack with both displayed bounds, up to numerical factors.</p><p>Freeze that stack and put \\(p=\\prod_i\\nu_{w_i}\\). The invertible map \\((X^s,\\Gamma)\\mapsto(X^s,Z)\\) gives\n\\[\nC(\\mathbf w)\n=\nD\\bigl((X^s,\\Gamma)\\|p\\times q\\bigr)+H(Z)-F_0.\n\\]\nLet \\(Q_Z\\) be the output law under \\(p\\times q\\). Data processing yields\n\\[\nC(\\mathbf w)+F_0\n\\ge D(\\mathcal L(Z)\\|Q_Z)+H(Z)\n=\\mathbb E[-\\log Q_Z(Z)].\n\\]\nSome output atom \\(z_*\\) therefore satisfies\n\\[\nQ_Z(z_*)\\ge q(0)e^{-CE}.\n\\]\nBy the characteristic formula,\n\\[\ne^{-CE}\n\\le\\frac{Q_Z(z_*)}{q(0)}\n=\n\\int\\chi_{gz_*}(\\theta)\\prod_i\n\\mathbb E_{\\nu_{w_i}}\\chi_{-gX}(\\theta)\\,d\\omega(\\theta)\n\\le\\int\\prod_i|\\varphi_i(\\theta)|\\,d\\omega(\\theta),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\varphi_i=\\mathbb E_{\\nu_{w_i}}\\chi_{gX}\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\nA(\\theta)=\\sum_i(1-|\\varphi_i(\\theta)|^2).\n\\]\nSince \\(\\prod_i|\\varphi_i|\\le e^{-A/2}\\), choosing a sufficiently large numerical \\(C_R\\) gives a region\n\\[\nR=\\{A\\le C_RE\\}\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n\\omega(R)\\ge e^{-CE},\n\\qquad\n\\int\\sum_i(1-|\\varphi_i|^2)\\,d\\omega_R\\le CE.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\omega_R\\) is normalized restriction. A uniform-coordinate selection, applying Markov to this bound and \\(B(\\mathbf w)\\), gives an index \\(i\\) such that\n\\[\n\\int(1-|\\varphi_i|^2)\\,d\\omega_R\\le CE/s,\n\\qquad\nD(\\nu_{w_i}\\|U_S)\\le\\frac{C(D_0)d^2}{Eh^2}.\n\\]\nWrite \\(\\mu=\\nu_{w_i}\\). For independent \\(X,\\breve X\\sim\\mu\\),\n\\[\n1-|\\varphi_i|^2\n=\n\\mathbb E[1-\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_{g(X-\\breve X)}].\n\\]\nAveraging selects \\(x_0\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\mu\\) with mean phase defect at most \\(CE/s\\). Pruning by Markov gives \\(S'\\subset\\operatorname{supp}\\mu\\), \\(\\mu(S')\\ge1/2\\), such that every \\(x\\in S'\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\int(1-\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_{g(x-x_0)})\\,d\\omega_R\\le CE/s.\n\\]\nIf the mean is zero, use its full-mass zero-defect set.</p><p>Let \\(t=\\mu(S')\\), \\(v_0=|S'|/|S|\\). Binary data processing gives\n\\[\nD(\\mu\\|U_S)\n\\ge t\\log(t/v_0)+(1-t)\\log((1-t)/(1-v_0))\n\\ge t\\log(1/v_0)-\\log2.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\log|S|-\\log|S'|\n\\le2D(\\mu\\|U_S)+2\\log2\n\\le\\frac{C(D_0)d^2}{Eh^2}.\n\\]</p><p>Finally, for every probability \\(v\\) on this fixed \\(S'\\), Jensen's inequality and \\(L\\le s\\) give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\int\\exp\\left[-L\\sum_{x\\in S'}v(x)\n(1-\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_{g(x-x_0)})\\right]\\,d\\omega\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad\\ge\n\\omega(R)\\exp\\left[\n-L\\sum_{x\\in S'}v(x)\n\\int(1-\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_{g(x-x_0)})\\,d\\omega_R\n\\right]\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad\\ge e^{-CE}e^{-CEL/s}\\ge e^{-CE}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe region, center, and subset were fixed before \\(v\\). The exponential constants are numerical; dependence on \\(D_0\\) enters only the entropy loss and large-\\(d\\) threshold.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**A noisy sum with prescribed marginals — original Lemma 3.2.** Under Proposition 1's assumptions, let \\(U_S\\) be uniform on \\(S\\).", "statement": "<p>**A noisy sum with prescribed marginals — original Lemma 3.2.**</p><p>Under Proposition 1's assumptions, let \\(U_S\\) be uniform on \\(S\\). There is a joint law of\n\\[\nX^T=(X_1,\\ldots,X_T),\\qquad \\Gamma\\in TS-TS\n\\]\nsuch that every \\(X_i\\) has law \\(U_S\\), the joint law is permutation-invariant in the letters, and, with\n\\[\nZ=\\sum_{i=1}^TX_i+\\Gamma,\n\\]\none has\n\\[\nD(X^T\\|U_S^{\\otimes T})+I(X^T;Z)+\\mathbb Ec(\\Gamma)\\le E.\n\\]\nThe construction may be made with \\(Z\\in TS\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>On the compact polytope of laws on \\(S^T\\times TS\\) with uniform one-letter marginals, minimize\n\\[\nTH(U_S)-H(X^T\\mid Z)\n+\\mathbb Ec\\left(Z-\\sum_iX_i\\right).\n\\]\nConditional entropy is continuous, including at the boundary, and concave in the joint law; hence the minimum is attained and the problem is convex.</p><p>For prescribed one-letter marginals \\(\\mu=(\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_T)\\), let \\(f(\\mu)\\) be the minimum of\n\\[\n-H(X^T\\mid Z)+\\mathbb Ec\\left(Z-\\sum_iX_i\\right).\n\\]\nIt is finite and convex on the product of simplices. At the tuple of uniform marginals, a relative interior point, it has a supporting affine function. Thus finite multipliers for the marginal constraints give the dual formula\n\\[\nTH(U_S)+\n\\sup_{a_1,\\ldots,a_T:S\\to\\mathbb R}\n\\left\\{\n\\sum_i\\mathbb E_{U_S}a_i\n-\\log\\max_{z\\in TS}\n\\sum_{x\\in S^T}e^{\\sum_i a_i(x_i)}\nH_q\\left(z-\\sum_ix_i\\right)\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nIndeed, with\n\\[\nB_z=\\sum_{x\\in S^T}e^{\\sum_i a_i(x_i)}\nH_q\\left(z-\\sum_ix_i\\right),\n\\]\nthe conditional entropy-and-cost functional, after subtracting the multipliers, is\n\\[\nD\\left(\\pi\\,\\middle\\|\\,B_z^{-1}e^{\\sum_i a_i(x_i)}\nH_q\\left(z-\\sum_ix_i\\right)\\right)-\\log B_z.\n\\]\nIts minimum is \\(-\\log B_z\\); optimizing the law of \\(Z\\) chooses a maximizer of \\(B_z\\).</p><p>The dual objective is concave and invariant under permutations of the multipliers. Averaging permits \\(a_i=a\\). Put\n\\[\nA_a=\\sum_{x\\in S}e^{a(x)},\\qquad\n\\lambda(x)=e^{a(x)}/A_a.\n\\]\nThe dual expression becomes\n\\[\n-TD(U_S\\|\\lambda)\n-\\log\\max_{z\\in TS}\n\\mathbb E_{\\lambda^{\\otimes T}}\nH_q\\left(z-\\sum_iX_i\\right).\n\\]\nThe maximum is at least its average when \\(z\\) is the sum of an independent \\(T\\)-tuple of law \\(\\lambda\\). Proposition 1's hypothesis makes this average at least \\(e^{-E}\\). Thus every dual value is at most \\(E\\).</p><p>Finally average a minimizing joint law over letter permutations, and set\n\\[\n\\Gamma=Z-\\sum_iX_i.\n\\]\nThe identity\n\\[\nD(X^T\\|U_S^{\\otimes T})+I(X^T;Z)\n=TH(U_S)-H(X^T\\mid Z)\n\\]\ngives the result, including exchangeability jointly with the error.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Projection and conditioning rules — original Lemma 3.3.** For a probability law \\(p\\) on a finite integer alphabet and a finite-valued law of \\((X^s,\\Gamma)\\), with every coordinate supported on …", "statement": "<p>**Projection and conditioning rules — original Lemma 3.3.**</p><p>For a probability law \\(p\\) on a finite integer alphabet and a finite-valued law of \\((X^s,\\Gamma)\\), with every coordinate supported on \\(\\operatorname{supp}p\\), define\n\\[\nC_p(X^s,\\Gamma)\n=\nD(X^s\\|p^{\\otimes s})+I(X^s;Z)+\\mathbb Ec(\\Gamma),\n\\qquad\nZ=\\sum_iX_i+\\Gamma.\n\\]\nEquivalently,\n\\[\nC_p\n=\n\\mathbb E[-\\log p^{\\otimes s}(X^s)]-H(X^s\\mid Z)+\\mathbb Ec(\\Gamma).\n\\]\nThis cost is nonnegative and convex in the joint law.</p><p>The following operations are permitted.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Keeping any fixed subset of letters, in any fixed order, and adding the same \\(\\Gamma\\) to their sum does not increase \\(C_p\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Conditioning on any finite-valued variable \\(J\\) increases average cost by at most \\(H(J)\\). Projection and reordering may then depend on \\(J\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Conditioning on a fixed prefix and keeping the remaining letters incurs no additional average cost. If their common conditional marginal is \\(\\nu\\), replacing the comparison product by \\(\\nu^{\\otimes k}\\) does not increase that conditional cost.</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed \\(p\\), convexity follows because the cross entropy and error cost are linear and \\(-H(X^s\\mid Z)\\) is convex.</p><p>For projection, write \\(B,O\\) for the kept and omitted vectors and\n\\[\nZ_B=Z-\\sum O.\n\\]\nSince \\(H(B\\mid Z_B)\\ge H(B\\mid Z,O)\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_p(B,O;\\Gamma)-C_p(B,\\Gamma)\n&amp;=\\mathbb E[-\\log p^{\\otimes|O|}(O)]\n-H(B,O\\mid Z)+H(B\\mid Z_B)\\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\mathbb E[-\\log p^{\\otimes|O|}(O)]-H(O\\mid Z)\\ge0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nReordering changes neither the sum nor the product comparison.</p><p>For conditioning,\n\\[\n\\sum_j\\mathbb P(J=j)C_p(X^s,\\Gamma\\mid J=j)-C_p(X^s,\\Gamma)\n=I(X^s;J\\mid Z)\\le H(J).\n\\]\nThis permits \\(J\\) to depend on the letters, the error, and auxiliary randomness.</p><p>If \\(B\\) is a revealed prefix and \\(O\\) the remaining vector, conditioning on \\(B\\) makes \\(Z-\\sum B\\) an invertible translation of \\(Z\\). Hence\n\\[\nC_p(B,O;\\Gamma)-\\mathbb E_BC_p(O,\\Gamma\\mid B)\n=\n\\mathbb E[-\\log p^{\\otimes|B|}(B)]-H(B\\mid Z)\\ge0.\n\\]\nAt a prefix where all remaining marginals are \\(\\nu\\), changing the product comparison decreases cross entropy by \\(|O|D(\\nu\\|p)\\). The other terms are unchanged.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Selection of a conditional marginal — original Lemma 3.4.** Under Proposition 1's assumptions, put \\(T_0=\\lfloor T/4\\rfloor\\).", "statement": "<p>**Selection of a conditional marginal — original Lemma 3.4.**</p><p>Under Proposition 1's assumptions, put \\(T_0=\\lfloor T/4\\rfloor\\). There are a probability law \\(\\nu\\) on \\(S\\) and a law \\(\\Psi\\) of an exchangeable \\(T_0\\)-tuple of marginal \\(\\nu\\), jointly with \\(\\Gamma\\in TS-TS\\), such that, for\n\\[\nZ=\\sum_{i=1}^{T_0}X_i+\\Gamma,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log|S|-H(\\nu)\\le CE/T,\n\\]\n\\[\nD(X^{T_0}\\|\\nu^{\\otimes T_0})+I(X^{T_0};Z)+\\mathbb Ec(\\Gamma)\\le CE,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\|\\mathcal L_\\Psi(X^m)-\\nu^{\\otimes m}\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n\\le C m_*\\sqrt E/T\n\\qquad(1\\le m\\le m_*).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Start with Lemma 1.1 and put\n\\[\ns_j=H(X_{j+1}\\mid X_1,\\ldots,X_j),\\qquad 0\\le j&lt;T.\n\\]\nExchangeability and conditioning imply that \\(s_j\\) is nonincreasing. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^{T-1}(\\log|S|-s_j)\n=D(X^T\\|U_S^{\\otimes T})\\le E,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n\\log|S|-s_j\\le E/(T-j).\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(a=\\lfloor T/4\\rfloor\\), \\(b=\\lfloor T/2\\rfloor\\), and choose \\(j\\) uniformly from \\([a,b]\\cap\\mathbb Z\\). Since \\(m_*=o(T)\\), all indices through \\(b+m_*\\) lie below \\(3T/4\\). The entropy deficits, and the total variation of the sequence \\(s_j\\) over this enlarged interval, are at most \\(CE/T\\).</p><p>For a positive-probability prefix \\(x^j\\), let \\(\\nu_{j,x^j}\\) be the common conditional marginal of the unrevealed letters. Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{X^j}\nD\\left(\n\\mathcal L(X_{j+1},\\ldots,X_{j+m}\\mid X^j)\n\\,\\middle\\|\\,\n\\nu_{j,X^j}^{\\otimes m}\n\\right)\n=\n\\sum_{i=0}^{m-1}(s_j-s_{j+i}).\n\\]\nFor \\(i&lt;m_*\\), summation over \\(j\\) cancels interior terms:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=a}^b(s_j-s_{j+i})\n=\n\\sum_{j=a}^{a+i-1}s_j-\\sum_{j=b+1}^{b+i}s_j\n\\le CiE/T.\n\\]\nConsequently the average divergence for \\(m=m_*\\) is at most\n\\[\nCm_*^2E/T^2.\n\\]</p><p>The average marginal deficit is at most \\(CE/T\\). By Lemma 1.2, conditioning on the prefix, projecting to \\(T_0\\) remaining letters, and changing comparison to their common conditional marginal has average noisy cost at most \\(E\\).</p><p>All three quantities are nonnegative. Normalize them by their upper scales and select one common prefix and length for which all three bounds hold with larger numerical constants. Conditional exchangeability gives \\(\\Psi\\). Pinsker's inequality gives the stated \\(m_*\\)-letter total-variation bound; projection gives all smaller \\(m\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Monotonicities and uniform entropy bounds — original Lemma 3.5.** Fix \\(\\nu,\\Psi\\) from Lemma 1.3.", "statement": "<p>**Monotonicities and uniform entropy bounds — original Lemma 3.5.**</p><p>Fix \\(\\nu,\\Psi\\) from Lemma 1.3. A context is a finite-valued variable \\(W\\) jointly distributed with \\(X\\sim\\nu\\). A refinement replaces \\(W\\) by \\((W,A)\\), where \\(A\\) is sampled by a finite channel conditional on \\((X,W)\\). The marginal of \\(X\\) remains \\(\\nu\\).</p><p>For \\(s\\) independent copies of the current pair, define\n\\[\nG_s(W)=H\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^sX_i\\,\\middle|\\,W_1,\\ldots,W_s\\right),\n\\qquad G_0(W)=0.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nI_r(W)\n=\n\\inf\\left\\{\nrH(X\\mid W)\n-\nH\\left(X_1,\\ldots,X_r\\,\\middle|\\,\\sum_{a=1}^rX_a,W_1,\\ldots,W_r\\right)\n\\right\\},\n\\]\nwhere the infimum is over arbitrary couplings with the prescribed one-row marginal \\((X,W)\\).</p><p>There is \\(D_*=C'_0(D_0)d\\) such that every finite refinement of the constant context satisfies:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(G_s\\) is nondecreasing in \\(s\\), nonincreasing under refinement, and \\(0\\le G_s\\le D_*\\) for \\(s\\le m_*\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\(I_r\\) is nonnegative, nondecreasing in \\(r\\), nonincreasing under refinement, and \\(I_r\\le D_*\\) for \\(r\\le T_0\\).</p><p>The same \\(D_*\\) works throughout every finite sequence of refinements.</p>", "proof": "<p>A general refinement calculation will be used repeatedly. Suppose a trial has prescribed row marginals \\((B_a,V_a)\\), output \\(Z\\), and cost\n\\[\nF=\\sum_aH(B_a\\mid V_a)-H(B^r\\mid Z,V^r)\\ge0.\n\\]\nRun independent channels \\(A_a\\) given the old pairs, independently of \\(Z\\) given all old pairs. The refined cost \\(F'\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nF-F'\n&amp;=\\sum_aI(B_a;A_a\\mid V_a)-I(B^r;A^r\\mid Z,V^r)\\\\\n&amp;=\\sum_aH(A_a\\mid V_a)-H(A^r\\mid Z,V^r)\\ge0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe second equality uses conditional independence.</p><p>By Lemma 1.2, every projection of \\(\\Psi\\) to \\(s\\le T_0\\) letters has \\(C_\\nu\\le CE\\). Relative entropy to \\(q\\) gives\n\\[\nH(\\Gamma)\\le F_0+\\mathbb Ec(\\Gamma).\n\\]\nFor \\(Z_s=\\sum_{i=1}^sX_i+\\Gamma\\),\n\\[\nH(Z_s)\\le I(X^s;Z_s)+H(\\Gamma).\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nH_\\Psi\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^sX_i\\right)\n\\le I(X^s;Z_s)+2H(\\Gamma)\\le2F_0+CE.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(s\\le m_*\\), the correlated and independent sum laws differ in total variation by at most\n\\[\n\\eta=Cm_*\\sqrt E/T.\n\\]\nFor laws on an alphabet of size \\(N\\), a coupling with disagreement probability \\(\\eta'\\) gives\n\\[\n|H(P)-H(Q)|\\le\\log2+\\eta'\\log N.\n\\]\nApply this on \\(sS\\). The added error is\n\\[\n\\log2+C\\frac{m_*\\sqrt E}{T}\\log|sS|\n=o(d/h^2),\n\\]\nbecause \\(m_*\\sqrt E/T\\le d^{-1/2+o(1)}\\) and the sumset hypothesis bounds \\(\\log|sS|\\). Thus \\(G_s(\\varnothing)\\le C(D_0)d\\).</p><p>Conditional on all contexts, the letters defining \\(G_s\\) are independent. For independent integer-valued \\(A,B\\),\n\\[\nH(A+B)\\ge H(A+B\\mid B)=H(A).\n\\]\nThis proves monotonicity in \\(s\\). Refinement decreases \\(G_s\\) by conditioning.</p><p>Nonnegativity of \\(I_r\\) follows from conditional subadditivity. For an \\((r+1)\\)-row trial,\n\\[\nH(X^{r+1}\\mid S_{r+1},W^{r+1})\n\\le H(X\\mid W)+H(X^r\\mid S_r,W^r),\n\\quad S_r=\\sum_{a=1}^rX_a,\n\\]\nwhich proves monotonicity in \\(r\\). Applying the general refinement calculation with \\(Z=S_r\\) proves refinement monotonicity.</p><p>Finally the projected \\(\\Psi\\) trial gives\n\\[\nI_r(\\varnothing)\n\\le D(X^r\\|\\nu^{\\otimes r})+H(S_r)\n\\le2F_0+CE.\n\\]\nChoose \\(C'_0(D_0)\\) large enough for both initial bounds, and use refinement monotonicity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Lifting block sums and refining contexts — original Lemma 3.6.** In Lemma 1.4's setting, let \\( Y_m=\\sum_{i=1}^mX_i,\\qquad V_m=(W_1,\\ldots,W_m) \\) for \\(m\\) independent current pairs.", "statement": "<p>**Lifting block sums and refining contexts — original Lemma 3.6.**</p><p>In Lemma 1.4's setting, let\n\\[\nY_m=\\sum_{i=1}^mX_i,\\qquad V_m=(W_1,\\ldots,W_m)\n\\]\nfor \\(m\\) independent current pairs. For positive integers \\(m,r\\), define\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nK_m(W;r)=\\inf\\bigg\\{\n&amp;rH(Y_m\\mid V_m)\n-H\\left(Y_{m,1},\\ldots,Y_{m,r}\\,\\middle|\\,\n\\sum_{a=1}^rY_{m,a}+\\Gamma,V_m^r\\right)\\\\\n&amp;+\\mathbb Ec(\\Gamma)\n\\bigg\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nEach row has prescribed marginal \\((Y_m,V_m)\\), rows may be arbitrarily coupled, and \\(\\Gamma\\in TS-TS\\).</p><p>The value is unchanged if each row sum in both entropy terms is replaced by its whole \\(m\\)-letter vector, with the row constrained to have the law of \\(m\\) independent current pairs. The output remains the sum of all \\(mr\\) letters plus \\(\\Gamma\\).</p><p>Moreover \\(K_m(W;r)\\) is nonincreasing under finite context refinement.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(B_a=(X_{a,1},\\ldots,X_{a,m})\\), \\(V_a=(W_{a,1},\\ldots,W_{a,m})\\), \\(Y_a=\\sum_iX_{a,i}\\), and \\(Z=\\sum_aY_a+\\Gamma\\). The full-vector objective minus its sum projection is\n\\[\n\\sum_aH(B_a\\mid Y_a,V_a)-H(B^r\\mid Y^r,Z,V^r)\\ge0.\n\\]</p><p>Conversely, from a sum trial, sample the full blocks independently conditional on their own sums and contexts:\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(B^r\\mid Y^r,V^r,\\Gamma)\n=\n\\prod_a\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{iid}}(B_a\\mid Y_a,V_a).\n\\]\nEach row has the required independent-block marginal. Conditioning further on \\(Z\\) preserves these conditional row laws, so the preceding difference is zero. This proves equality of infima.</p><p>For refinement, use the full-vector formulation and run the one-letter refinement channel independently at every position, also independently of the old error given the old pairs. This preserves each row's prescribed independent refined-pair marginal. Lemma 1.4's refinement calculation decreases the entropy objective and leaves the error cost unchanged.</p><p>If the one-letter channel is specified using auxiliary letters, it is run with fresh auxiliary randomness at each position. Actual letters elsewhere in the row are not substituted for those auxiliary samples.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**The low-end noisy cost — original Lemma 3.7.** Under Proposition 1's assumptions, fix \\(\\nu\\) from Lemma 1.3.", "statement": "<p>**The low-end noisy cost — original Lemma 3.7.**</p><p>Under Proposition 1's assumptions, fix \\(\\nu\\) from Lemma 1.3. If\n\\[\nm\\le m_*,\n\\qquad\n2mr\\le T_0,\n\\qquad\nr\\le\\tfrac12Te^{-w^2},\n\\]\nthen, for the constant context and every finite refinement,\n\\[\nK_m(W;r)\\le CE.\n\\]\nThe constant is numerical; the threshold for \\(d\\) may depend on \\(D_0,P_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>It suffices by Lemma 1.5 to construct a constant-context trial. Take \\(2r\\) disjoint \\(m\\)-blocks from \\(\\Psi\\), with common marginal \\(\\mu\\), and put \\(Q=\\nu^{\\otimes m}\\). Then\n\\[\n\\varepsilon=\\|\\mu-Q\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\n\\le Cm_*\\sqrt E/T.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\gamma=\\min(\\mu,Q)\\). Tag a block of value \\(x\\) with probability \\(\\gamma(x)/\\mu(x)\\), independently given all block values. Tagged blocks have unnormalized law \\(\\gamma\\).</p><p>Let \\(B\\) be the number tagged. Reject if \\(B&lt;r\\). Otherwise accept with probability \\(B/(2r)\\), and on acceptance choose \\(r\\) distinct tagged blocks uniformly in random order. If acceptance probability is \\(1-\\delta\\), then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(2r-B)=2r\\varepsilon,\\qquad\n\\mathbb P(B&lt;r)\\le2\\varepsilon,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\delta\n=\n\\varepsilon+\\mathbb E\\left[\\frac B{2r}\\mathbf1_{\\{B&lt;r\\}}\\right]\n\\le3\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nThe acceptance weight cancels the \\(1/B\\) chance that a tagged block occupies a specified row. Thus every selected row has the same unnormalized marginal\n\\[\n\\eta(x)\n=\n\\frac1{2r}\\sum_{i=1}^{2r}\n\\mathbb E[\n\\mathbf1_{\\{B\\ge r\\}}\\mathbf1_{\\{i\\text{ tagged}\\}}\n\\mathbf1_{\\{\\text{block }i=x\\}}\n]\n\\le\\gamma(x)\\le Q(x),\n\\]\nof total mass \\(1-\\delta\\).</p><p>Record only a rejection symbol or the ordered selected indices in \\(J\\), not all tags. Then\n\\[\nH(J)\\le\\log2+r\\log(2r).\n\\]\nProjection, conditioning on \\(J\\), and reordering by Lemma 1.2, followed by convexity when indices are forgotten, give\n\\[\n(1-\\delta)C_\\nu(P_{\\mathrm{acc}})\n\\le CE+\\log2+r\\log(2r).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), fill the missing mass using \\(r\\) independent blocks of law\n\\[\nR=(Q-\\eta)/\\delta,\n\\]\nwith error zero. Since \\(R\\le Q/\\delta\\),\n\\[\nD(R\\|Q)\\le\\log(1/\\delta).\n\\]\nThe filling output lies in \\(mrS\\), so its weighted additional cost is at most\n\\[\n\\delta r\\log(1/\\delta)+\\delta\\log|mrS|.\n\\]\nThe resulting mixture has exactly marginal \\(Q\\) in every row and is admissible by Lemma 1.5.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\nr\\log(2r)\n\\le Cdh^{-5}e^{-\\sqrt{\\log d}}\\log d=o(d/h^2),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\delta r\\log(1/\\delta)\\le r/e=o(d/h^2),\n\\]\nand, since \\(\\delta\\le d^{-1/2+o(1)}\\) and \\(mr&lt;d^2\\),\n\\[\n\\delta\\log|mrS|\n\\le d^{-1/2+o(1)}D_0dh^{P_0}\\log d=o(d/h^2).\n\\]\nBecause \\(E\\ge d/h^2\\), these terms are absorbed into \\(CE\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.7", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**A block observation pays a noisy-cost difference — original Lemma 4.1.** Fix a current finite context \\(W\\), integers \\(m\\ge2\\), \\(r\\ge1\\), and an independent block of \\(m\\) current pairs.", "statement": "<p>**A block observation pays a noisy-cost difference — original Lemma 4.1.**</p><p>Fix a current finite context \\(W\\), integers \\(m\\ge2\\), \\(r\\ge1\\), and an independent block of \\(m\\) current pairs. Refine the context of \\(X_1\\), retaining \\(W_1\\), by observing\n\\[\n\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^mX_i,W_2,\\ldots,W_m\\right).\n\\]\nCall the resulting context \\(W'\\). Then\n\\[\nG_1(W)-G_1(W')=G_m(W)-G_{m-1}(W),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nI_r(W)-I_r(W')\n\\ge K_m(W;r)-K_{m-1}(W;r).\n\\]\nIf the observation is made only on an independent Bernoulli mark of probability \\(\\zeta\\), with the mark retained, the one-letter entropy loss is multiplied by \\(\\zeta\\), and the decrease of \\(I_r\\) is at least \\(\\zeta(I_r(W)-I_r(W'))\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Independence gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;I\\left(X_1;\\sum_{i=1}^mX_i,W_2,\\ldots,W_m\\mid W_1\\right)\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad=\nH\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^mX_i\\mid W_1,\\ldots,W_m\\right)\n-\nH\\left(\\sum_{i=2}^mX_i\\mid W_2,\\ldots,W_m\\right),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhich proves the first identity.</p><p>Take independent minimizing trials for \\(I_r(W)\\) and \\(K_{m-1}(W;r)\\). Denote their letter or block-sum vectors by \\(U,Y\\), their context vectors by \\(\\mathbf W,V\\), and their outputs by\n\\[\nZ_U=\\sum_aU_a,\\qquad Z_Y=\\sum_aY_a+\\Gamma.\n\\]\nPut \\(P=(\\mathbf W,V)\\). The trial \\((U+Y,P,\\Gamma)\\) is admissible for \\(K_m(W;r)\\), while \\((U,(U+Y,P))\\), with exact output \\(Z_U\\), is admissible for \\(I_r(W')\\).</p><p>The positive one-row entropy terms add correctly because\n\\[\nH(U_a+Y_a\\mid W_a,V_a)\n+\nH(U_a\\mid U_a+Y_a,W_a,V_a)\n=\nH(U_a\\mid W_a)+H(Y_a\\mid V_a).\n\\]\nFor the joint subtracted terms,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;H(U+Y\\mid Z_U+Z_Y,P)\n+H(U\\mid U+Y,Z_U,P)\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\\ge\nH(U+Y\\mid Z_U,Z_Y,P)\n+H(U\\mid U+Y,Z_U,Z_Y,P)\\\\\n&amp;\\quad=\nH(U,Y\\mid Z_U,Z_Y,P)\\\\\n&amp;\\quad=\nH(U\\mid Z_U,\\mathbf W)+H(Y\\mid Z_Y,V).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe middle equality uses the invertible map \\((U,Y)\\mapsto(U+Y,U)\\); the last uses independence. The error cost is unchanged. Taking infima gives the second inequality.</p><p>For a partial observation, synchronize the mark across rows and mix an optimal old trial with an optimal fully refined trial. This gives\n\\[\nI_r(W^{(\\zeta)})\n\\le(1-\\zeta)I_r(W)+\\zeta I_r(W'),\n\\]\nwhile conditioning on the mark gives the asserted entropy loss.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.8", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Finite refinement search — original Proposition 4.2.** Use the costs of Lemmas 1.4–1.7 and enlarge \\(D_*=C'_0(D_0)d\\) so that \\(E\\le D_*\\).", "statement": "<p>**Finite refinement search — original Proposition 4.2.**</p><p>Use the costs of Lemmas 1.4–1.7 and enlarge \\(D_*=C'_0(D_0)d\\) so that \\(E\\le D_*\\). Put\n\\[\nQ=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{w^3/2-w^2}{3w}\\right\\rfloor,\\qquad M=Q+1,\n\\]\nand, for \\(1\\le j\\le M\\),\n\\[\nm_{j,0}=\\left\\lceil\\exp\\bigl(w^2+3w(M-j)\\bigr)\\right\\rceil,\n\\quad\nr_j=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{T_0}{2m_{j,0}}\\right\\rfloor,\n\\quad\nm_{j,1}=\\left\\lceil\\frac L{r_j}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nThere are a finite sequence of context refinements from the constant context to \\(W\\), and an index \\(j\\), such that\n\\[\nG_1(\\varnothing)-G_1(W)\\le\\frac{CD_*^2}{Eh^2},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nK_{m_{j,1}}(W;r_j)\n\\le K_{m_{j,0}}(W;r_j)+E.\n\\]\nEvery context produced is finite-valued.</p>", "proof": "<p>The block ranges are disjoint and descend with \\(j\\), while \\(r_j\\) increases. For large \\(d\\),\n\\[\ne^{w^2}\\le m_{j,0}&lt;m_{j,1}\\le m_*,\n\\quad\n2r_jm_{j,0}\\le T_0,\n\\quad\nr_j\\le Te^{-w^2}/8,\n\\]\n\\[\nL\\le r_jm_{j,1}&lt;2L&lt;d^2,\n\\qquad\nm_{j,1}/m_{j,0}\\le e^{2w}.\n\\]\nThus Lemma 1.6 applies at every low endpoint.</p><p>Normalize\n\\[\na_j=I_{r_j}/D_*,\n\\qquad\ng_s=G_s/D_*,\n\\qquad\n\\tau=E/D_*\\in(0,1].\n\\]\nAll values lie in \\([0,1]\\); \\(a_j\\) increases in \\(j\\), \\(g_s\\) increases in \\(s\\), and both decrease under refinement. Charge a refinement \\(\\tau\\) times its loss in \\(g_1\\). Total cost \\(c\\) means entropy loss \\(cD_*^2/E\\).</p><p>If the desired \\(K\\)-inequality fails at \\(j\\), consider, separately from that state, all full observations from Lemma 1.7 for \\(m_{j,0}&lt;m\\le m_{j,1}\\). Their decreases \\(d_m\\) in \\(a_j\\) and costs \\(c_m\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\sum_md_m&gt;\\tau,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_mc_m=\\tau p_j,\n\\qquad\np_j=g_{m_{j,1}}-g_{m_{j,0}}.\n\\]\nOffers with \\(c_m&gt;2p_jd_m\\) contribute less than \\(\\tau/2\\) total decrease. Hence some remaining offer satisfies\n\\[\nd_m\\ge\\eta:=\\tau/(2m_*),\n\\qquad c_m\\le2p_jd_m.\n\\]\nIf \\(p_j=0\\), all costs vanish and the same conclusion holds. A partial observation targeting \\(x\\le d_m\\) decreases \\(a_j\\) by at least \\(x\\), at cost at most \\(2p_jx\\).</p><p>Set\n\\[\nN=\\lfloor M^{1/10}\\rfloor,\\quad n=\\lfloor M^{1/2}\\rfloor,\n\\quad u=(\\log N)^{-2},\\quad q_*=\\lceil2/u\\rceil,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha_0=\\frac{100u}{\\log n},\\quad\\delta_0=n^{-1/2},\n\\qquad\n\\alpha_1=\\frac{10^5}{\\log n\\log N},\\quad\\delta_1=N^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nFor large \\(d\\), \\(N\\ge4\\) and \\(Nq_*n\\le M\\). Partition the first \\(Nq_*n\\) indices into \\(N\\) successive macroblocks, each with \\(q_*\\) successive subblocks of length \\(n\\). Visit macroblocks in increasing order, subblocks inside each macroblock in decreasing order, and indices inside each subblock in increasing order.</p><p>At index \\(j\\), let \\(c_0\\) be cost spent there and\n\\[\np=g_{m_{j,1}}(\\text{entry})-g_{m_{j,0}}(\\text{current}).\n\\]\nSkip the index if\n\\[\np&gt;c_0/\\alpha_0+\\delta_0.\n\\]\nOtherwise test the desired inequality; if it fails, take an offer with target\n\\[\nx=\\min\\{d_m,u-\\Delta_j\\},\n\\qquad\n\\Delta_j=a_j(\\text{entry})-a_j(\\text{current}).\n\\]\nContinue until a skip, discovery, or decrease at least \\(u\\). The total target sum is at most \\(u\\); every unclipped target is at least \\(\\eta\\), so the loop is finite. The failed skip test gives\n\\[\nc_{0,\\mathrm{new}}+\\alpha_0\\delta_0\n\\le(c_{0,\\mathrm{old}}+\\alpha_0\\delta_0)(1+2x/\\alpha_0).\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nc_0\\le\\alpha_0\\delta_0(e^{2u/\\alpha_0}-1)\n\\le\\alpha_0n^{-12/25}\\le\\alpha_0n^{-2/5}.\n\\]</p><p>For a macroblock with highest and lowest block-length endpoints \\(H,\\ell\\), put\n\\[\nP=g_H(\\text{macroblock entry})-g_\\ell(\\text{current})\\in[0,1].\n\\]\nSuccessive skipped indices \\(j&lt;j'\\) inside a subblock satisfy\n\\[\ng_{m_{j',1}}(\\text{entry to }j')\n\\le g_{m_{j,0}}(\\text{exit from }j).\n\\]\nTheir skip widths therefore sum to at most \\(P\\). Each skip has \\(p&gt;\\delta_0\\), so fewer than \\(1/\\delta_0=\\sqrt n\\) indices can be skipped. Every prefix of a subblock costs at most\n\\[\n\\alpha_0(P+n^{-2/5}).\n\\]\nA subblock without discovery must consequently complete a target.</p><p>At subblock boundaries, let \\(c_1\\) be accumulated macroblock cost and skip the rest of the macroblock if\n\\[\nP&gt;c_1/\\alpha_1+\\delta_1.\n\\]\nTarget indices decrease between completed subblocks. Since \\(a_j\\) increases in \\(j\\) and decreases in time,\n\\[\na_{j'}(\\text{entry to }j')\\le a_j(\\text{exit from }j)\n\\qquad(j'&gt;\\!0,\\ j'&lt;j).\n\\]\nCompleted decreases telescope to at most one. Thus at most \\(1/u\\) subblocks can complete targets, fewer than \\(q_*\\); a macroblock must be skipped or discover the desired inequality.</p><p>For a completed continuing subblock,\n\\[\nc_{1,\\mathrm{new}}-c_{1,\\mathrm{old}}\n\\le\\alpha_0(c_{1,\\mathrm{new}}/\\alpha_1+\\delta_1+n^{-2/5}).\n\\]\nWith\n\\[\n\\beta_*=\\alpha_0/\\alpha_1=\\frac1{1000\\log N},\n\\qquad\n\\delta_*=\\delta_1+n^{-2/5},\n\\]\niteration over at most \\(1/u\\) runs gives\n\\[\nc_1\\le\\alpha_1\\delta_*\n\\left((1-\\beta_*)^{-1/u}-1\\right)\\le\\alpha_1.\n\\]\nHere \\(n\\ge N^5\\), \\(n^{-2/5}\\le N^{-2}\\), and\n\\[\n(1-\\beta_*)^{-1/u}\\le N^{1/500}.\n\\]\nA last or partial subblock costs at most \\(2\\alpha_0\\).</p><p>Across skipped macroblocks the widths \\(P\\) telescope to at most one. Their total cost is at most \\(\\alpha_1\\), and fewer than \\(1/\\delta_1=\\sqrt N\\) macroblocks can be skipped. Hence discovery occurs, with total cost at most\n\\[\n\\alpha_1+\\alpha_1+2\\alpha_0\\le4\\alpha_1.\n\\]\nBoth telescopes permit decreases caused by intervening refinements. Since \\(h=4\\log w\\), \\(M\\asymp w^2\\), and \\(\\log n,\\log N\\asymp h\\), this is \\(O(h^{-2})\\). Undoing normalization proves the entropy bound. Every observation and Bernoulli mark has a finite alphabet.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Thermal positivity, form order, and spatial estimates — original Definition 5.1 and Lemma 5.2, with the estimates following them.** Work on the unit circle with normalized measure.", "statement": "<p>**Thermal positivity, form order, and spatial estimates — original Definition 5.1 and Lemma 5.2, with the estimates following them.**</p><p>Work on the unit circle with normalized measure. A thermal form is\n\\[\nA(y)=\\sum_s\\lambda_s(1-\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_s(y)),\n\\qquad \\lambda_s\\ge0,\n\\]\nwith finitely many terms. Write \\(A\\preceq A'\\) if \\(A'-A\\) is thermal, and define\n\\[\nq_A(u)=\\int e^{-A(y)}\\chi_u(y)\\,dy,\\quad\nF(A)=-\\log q_A(0),\\quad\nK_A=e^{F(A)-A},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Delta_A(u)\n=\n\\left(\\int|1-\\chi_u|^2K_A\\right)^{1/2},\n\\qquad\nB_A(r)=\\{u\\in\\mathbb Z:\\Delta_A(u)\\le r\\}.\n\\]</p><p>Then:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(q_A\\) is an even, nonnegative probability mass function on \\(\\mathbb Z\\), and\n   \\[\n   \\int\\chi_uK_A=q_A(u)/q_A(0)\\ge0.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For integers \\(u,v\\),\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{Cov}_{K_A}(\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_u,\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_v)\\ge0.\n   \\]\n   Hence the characteristic coefficients increase in form order, \\(\\Delta_A(u)\\) decreases in form order,\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_A(u+v)\\le\\Delta_A(u)+\\Delta_A(v),\n   \\]\n   and \\(F\\) increases in form order. For thermal \\(D\\), the increment \\(F(A+D)-F(A)\\) decreases as \\(A\\) increases in form order.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(P=\\sum_jP_j\\) is thermal and \\(P_{\\mathrm{lo}}\\preceq P\\preceq P_{\\mathrm{hi}}\\), then, for arbitrary shifts \\(z_j\\),\n   \\[\n   \\left|\\int\\chi_u(y)\\prod_je^{-P_j(z_j-y)}\\,dy\\right|\n   \\le q_P(u)\n   \\le e^{-F(P_{\\mathrm{lo}})}\\int\\chi_uK_{P_{\\mathrm{hi}}}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. For a real interval \\(p\\) of length at most one, put\n   \\[\n   R_p=|p|^{-1},\\qquad S_p=R_p^2(1-\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_1).\n   \\]\n   Then\n   \\[\n   F(S_p)=\\log R_p+O(1),\n   \\qquad\n   \\int(1-\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_1)K_{A+S_p}\\le CR_p^{-2},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   q_{S_p}(u)\\ge cR_p^{-1}\\quad(|u|\\le cR_p),\n   \\qquad\n   \\Delta_{A+S_p}(u)\\le C|u|/R_p.\n   \\]\n   In particular, \\(\\int S_pK_{A+S_p}\\le C\\). The same statements hold with any larger spatial scaling.</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand\n\\[\ne^{-\\lambda(1-\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_s)}\n=\ne^{-\\lambda}\\exp((\\lambda/2)\\chi_s)\\exp((\\lambda/2)\\chi_{-s}).\n\\]\nIts Fourier coefficients are nonnegative and sum to one. The expansions, and their finite products, are absolutely summable. Evaluation at zero gives \\(\\sum_uq_A(u)=1\\), and evenness gives \\(q_A(-u)=q_A(u)\\).</p><p>For covariance, write \\(c_s(y)=\\cos(2\\pi sy)\\) and\n\\[\nZ_A=\\int\\exp\\left(\\sum_s\\lambda_sc_s\\right).\n\\]\nExpress covariance with two independent copies as half the expectation of the product of their differences. The map\n\\[\n(x,z)\\mapsto(x+z,x-z)\n\\]\npushes Haar probability on the two-dimensional torus to Haar probability. The cosine differences are \\(-2\\sin(2\\pi ux)\\sin(2\\pi uz)\\), and the product weight becomes\n\\[\n\\exp\\left(2\\sum_s\\lambda_sc_s(x)c_s(z)\\right).\n\\]\nExpansion gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Cov}_{K_A}(c_u,c_v)\n=\n\\frac2{Z_A^2}\n\\sum_{(n_s)\\ge0}\n\\left(\\prod_s\\frac{(2\\lambda_s)^{n_s}}{n_s!}\\right)\n\\left(\n\\int\\sin(2\\pi ux)\\sin(2\\pi vx)\\prod_sc_s(x)^{n_s}\\,dx\n\\right)^2\n\\ge0.\n\\]\nAbsolute convergence justifies the expansion.</p><p>Differentiation gives\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial\\lambda_v}\\int c_uK_A\n=\\operatorname{Cov}_{K_A}(c_u,c_v),\n\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial\\lambda_u}\n=\\int(1-c_u)K_A.\n\\]\nThe latter derivatives are nonnegative and decrease in every nonnegative coefficient direction. Integration proves the assertions about form order and increments. Since\n\\[\n\\Delta_A(u)^2=2\\left(1-\\int c_uK_A\\right),\n\\]\nmetric monotonicity follows. Subadditivity follows from\n\\[\n1-\\chi_{u+v}=(1-\\chi_u)+\\chi_u(1-\\chi_v)\n\\]\nand the \\(L^2(K_A)\\) triangle inequality.</p><p>Translation multiplies Fourier coefficients by unit complex numbers. Absolute values in their convolution are bounded by the centered convolution \\(q_P(u)\\). Finally,\n\\[\nq_P(u)=e^{-F(P)}\\int\\chi_uK_P,\n\\]\nand form monotonicity gives the stated envelope.</p><p>For the spatial estimates, with \\(\\|y\\|\\) denoting distance to the nearest integer,\n\\[\n8\\|y\\|^2\\le1-\\cos(2\\pi y)\\le2\\pi^2\\|y\\|^2.\n\\]\nGaussian comparison gives\n\\[\n\\int e^{-S_p}\\asymp R_p^{-1},\n\\qquad\n\\int(1-\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_1)K_{S_p}\\le CR_p^{-2}.\n\\]\nForm monotonicity gives the latter bound for \\(A+S_p\\). The inequality\n\\[\n|1-\\chi_u|\\le|u||1-\\chi_1|\n\\]\ngives the metric estimate and, for sufficiently small \\(|u|/R_p\\), gives\n\\[\n\\int\\chi_uK_{S_p}\\ge1/2.\n\\]\nThis proves the remaining lower bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Spectral update — original Lemma 5.3.** Let \\(h=\\log\\log d\\), and retain \\(T,L,w\\) from Proposition 1.", "statement": "<p>**Spectral update — original Lemma 5.3.**</p><p>Let \\(h=\\log\\log d\\), and retain \\(T,L,w\\) from Proposition 1. Choose dyadic \\(b_0\\) with\n\\[\nh^{-16}/2&lt;b_0\\le h^{-16}.\n\\]\nComputation levels are dyadic \\(b\\in[b_0/8,1]\\), and\n\\[\nk_b=A_0\\frac dh(1+\\log(1/b))^2,\n\\qquad\nr_{\\mathrm{sep}}=h^{-30},\n\\qquad\n\\sigma=h^{-40}.\n\\]</p><p>A type-\\(A\\) label is \\(Z=Y+\\theta\\) modulo one, where \\(\\theta\\) has density \\(K_A\\). Channel parameters and all membership tests are chosen from spatial prefixes and earlier input membership outcomes, never from sampled labels.</p><p>Fix caps \\(C_0,C_*\\ge1\\). Suppose \\(A\\) arises from zero by fewer than \\(C_0h\\) additions of spatial forms and metric forms \\(D_i\\), each satisfying\n\\[\nF(A_{\\mathrm{base},i}+h^{140}D_i)\n-F(A_{\\mathrm{base},i}+D_i/2)\n\\le dh^{-400},\n\\]\nwhere \\(A_{\\mathrm{base},i}\\) includes that addition's spatial term. Let \\(p\\subset p_*\\), where \\(p_*\\) is the latest addition interval, or the top interval of length in \\([1/2,1]\\) before any addition. Assume\n\\[\nF(A)\\le\\log R_{p_*}+C_*d.\n\\]</p><p>On \\(p\\), let\n\\[\n0\\le a\\le e^d,\\qquad\ne^{-d}\\le m=\\mathbb E_pa\\le2\\alpha,\\qquad\\alpha&gt;0,\n\\]\nand let \\(P\\) have density \\(a/m\\) relative to uniform probability on \\(p\\). For a prospective child \\(p'\\), set\n\\[\nM_{p'}(z)=\\mathbb E_{p'}[aK_A(z-Y)]/\\alpha,\n\\quad\nf^u_{p'}(z)=\\mathbb E_{p'}[a\\chi_u(Y)K_A(z-Y)]/\\alpha,\n\\]\n\\[\nG^u_{p'}=\\int|f^u_{p'}|^2/M_{p'}.\n\\]\nAn integer is high at \\(b\\) if some \\(G^u_{p'}\\ge b^2\\). An update is requested if there are \\(\\lceil e^{k_b}\\rceil\\) high integers whose pairwise differences avoid\n\\[\nB_A(r_{\\mathrm{sep}})\n+\\{j\\in\\mathbb Z:|j|\\le\\sigma R_p\\}.\n\\]</p><p>There are numerical \\(C_1,C_2\\) and a sufficiently small numerical \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) such that the following holds. After fixing the caps, choose fixed sufficiently large \\(D_0,P_0\\), then \\(A_0\\), and finally sufficiently large \\(d\\). A finite deterministic construction supplies\n\\[\nd/h^2\\le t\\le D_0d,\\qquad t\\ge\\varepsilon k_b,\n\\]\na metric form \\(D_i=\\lambda B\\), and an input-only success set. On success,\n\\[\nA^+=A+S_p+D_i,\n\\]\n\\[\nF(A^+)-F(A+S_p)\\le C_1t,\n\\quad\n\\int BK_{A^+}\\le C_1t/\\lambda,\n\\quad\n\\lambda\\ge dh^{200},\n\\quad\nh^{140}\\lambda\\le L,\n\\]\nand the displayed flat-increment condition holds for \\(D_i\\).</p><p>There are two alternatives.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. A regular test has success probability \\(\\delta\\ge\\beta/2\\), \\(\\beta=b^2/100\\), and supplies a probability \\(\\nu\\) on a set \\(S'\\), a center \\(x_0\\), and\n   \\[\n   E_T(\\nu)\\ge t/4.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. A cheap update succeeds deterministically, has \\(t=\\varepsilon k_b\\), and uses uniform \\(\\nu\\) on \\(S'\\).</p><p>In both cases\n\\[\n\\log|S'|\\ge0.8k_b,\n\\qquad\nB=\\sum_x\\nu(x)(1-\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_{g(x-x_0)}),\n\\]\nwhere \\(g\\) is the snapping grid specified in Lemma 3.2.</p><p>Track a radius starting at \\(r_{\\mathrm{sep}}/4\\), increasing by\n\\[\n\\eta=r_{\\mathrm{sep}}/(10C_0h)\n\\]\nat every main addition. Through the count cap, the gap\n\\[\nF(A)-\\log|B_A(r)|\n\\]\nstarts at zero, is bounded below by a numerical negative constant, increases by at most \\(C_2t\\) at a regular success, and decreases by at least \\(k_b/2\\) at a cheap success. A failure makes no main addition and draws no label.</p>", "proof": "<p>On \\(S\\), and subsequently on its restrictions, put\n\\[\nt=\\max\\left(\\varepsilon k_b,\\max_{\\nu\\text{ supported on the current set}}E_T(\\nu)\\right).\n\\]\nThe maximum exists by compactness and strict positivity of the moment integral. For large \\(d\\), \\(d/h^2\\le t\\le D_0d\\). Apply Proposition 1 with \\(E=t\\), obtaining \\(S'\\) and \\(x_0\\).</p><p>If \\(\\max_{\\nu\\text{ on }S'}E_T(\\nu)\\ge t/4\\), keep a maximizing witness and make a regular test. If this maximum is less than \\(t/4\\) and \\(t=\\varepsilon k_b\\), use uniform \\(\\nu\\) and make a cheap update. Otherwise replace the current set by \\(S'\\) and repeat. Every nonterminal step reduces \\(t\\) by a factor at least four, except possibly the final transition to the floor. The process is finite and its total logarithmic cardinality loss is at most\n\\[\nC(D_0)\\frac{d^2}{\\varepsilon k_bh^2}.\n\\]\nIncluding the common-child loss \\(\\log2\\), choosing \\(A_0\\) large gives\n\\(\\log|S'|\\ge0.8k_b\\).</p><p>For the final \\(B\\), compression gives\n\\[\nF(\\widetilde A+LB)-F(\\widetilde A)\\le Ct.\n\\]\nWith \\(A_{\\mathrm{base}}=A+S_p\\), form monotonicity and Lemma 3.1 imply\n\\[\nF(A_{\\mathrm{base}}+LB)-F(A_{\\mathrm{base}})\\le C_1t.\n\\]\nThe cap-dependent tightening error is \\(o(t)\\) after constants are fixed.</p><p>Set\n\\[\n\\lambda_j=dh^{200}(2h^{140})^j,\\qquad\nN=\\left\\lfloor\n\\frac{\\log(2L/(dh^{200}))}{\\log(2h^{140})}\n\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nThe intervals \\([\\lambda_j/2,h^{140}\\lambda_j]\\), \\(0\\le j&lt;N\\), are adjacent and lie below \\(L\\). Since\n\\[\nN\\asymp e^{h/4}/\\log h&gt;C_1D_0h^{400}\n\\]\nfor sufficiently large \\(d\\), at least one interval has free-energy increment at most \\(dh^{-400}\\). Use that \\(\\lambda\\) and \\(D_i=\\lambda B\\). Concavity gives\n\\[\n\\int BK_{A_{\\mathrm{base}}+\\lambda B}\n\\le\n\\frac{F(A_{\\mathrm{base}}+\\lambda B)-F(A_{\\mathrm{base}})}{\\lambda}\n\\le C_1t/\\lambda.\n\\]</p><p>For a regular test, success is the input-only event\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\sum_x\\nu(x)\\chi_{gx}(Y)\\mathbb E[v_x(Z)\\mid Y]\n\\right|\\ge\\beta/2.\n\\]\nIts expression has modulus at most one and expected modulus at least \\(\\beta\\), so its probability is at least \\(\\beta/2\\). Cheap success is the whole input.</p><p>For the gap, while \\(r\\le r_{\\mathrm{sep}}/2\\),\n\\[\n|B_A(r)|e^{-F(A)}(1-r^2/2)\\le1,\n\\]\nso the gap is bounded below. Initially \\(A=0\\), \\(B_0(r)=\\{0\\}\\), and the gap is zero.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nU=B_A(r),\\qquad\nD_p=\\mathbb Z\\cap[-\\sigma R_p/10,\\sigma R_p/10],\n\\]\n\\[\n\\ell=|(U-U)\\cap(D_p-D_p)|\\ge1.\n\\]\nConvolution and Proposition 2 give\n\\[\nF(A+S_p)-F(A)\\le\\log R_p-\\log\\ell+C,\n\\qquad\n|U+D_p|\\ge|U||D_p|/\\ell.\n\\]\nThe same overlap multiplicity \\(\\ell\\) appears in both estimates.</p><p>Monotonicity and \\(\\Delta_{A^+}(j)\\le C|j|/R_p\\) imply\n\\[\nU+D_p\\subset B_{A^+}(r+\\eta/2).\n\\]\nFor cheap success, uniformity and the \\(B\\)-energy bound show that at least half the \\(x\\in S'\\) have\n\\[\n\\Delta_{A^+}(g(x-x_0))\\le C\\sqrt{t/\\lambda}=o(\\eta).\n\\]\nTheir translates \\(g(x-x_0)+U+D_p\\) lie in \\(B_{A^+}(r+\\eta)\\) and are disjoint: a collision, after restoring snapping errors, would violate the original separation.</p><p>Since \\(R_p/|D_p|\\le C/\\sigma\\), regular gap increase is at most\n\\[\nC_1t+O(\\log h)\\le C_2t.\n\\]\nCheap gap increase is at most\n\\[\nC_1\\varepsilon k_b-\\log(|S'|/2)+O(\\log h)\\le-k_b/2\n\\]\nfor sufficiently small numerical \\(\\varepsilon\\), then sufficiently large \\(d\\). These numerical choices precede the later caps.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Tightening and own-error energies — original Section 5.2.** Under Proposition 3's count, flat-increment, form-cap, and spatial-containment assumptions, choose a sufficiently large numerical \\(C_s\\) and put …", "statement": "<p>**Tightening and own-error energies — original Section 5.2.**</p><p>Under Proposition 3's count, flat-increment, form-cap, and spatial-containment assumptions, choose a sufficiently large numerical \\(C_s\\) and put\n\\[\n\\widetilde A=h^{130}A+C_sS_p.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nF(\\widetilde A)-F(A+S_p)\n\\le C+CC_0h(dh^{-400}+C\\log h),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int AK_A\\le CC_0h(dh^{-400}+1),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nF(A+S_p)\\le F(A)+\\log(R_p/R_{p_*})+C.\n\\]\nThe energy bound remains valid immediately after a final allowed addition that breaches a later stopping cap.</p>", "proof": "<p>Enlarge constituent forms one at a time. By decreasing increments, tightening an old metric term costs at most its flat-increment bound: the other current terms contain its original base form. Tightening an old spatial term costs\n\\[\nF(h^{130}S_{p_i})-F(S_{p_i})\\le C(1+\\log h)\n\\]\nby Proposition 2. Tightening the new \\(S_p\\) to \\(C_sS_p\\) costs \\(O(1)\\).</p><p>Concavity along a metric direction gives\n\\[\n\\int D_iK_A\n\\le2\\{F(A_{\\mathrm{base},i}+D_i)-F(A_{\\mathrm{base},i}+D_i/2)\\}\n\\le2dh^{-400}.\n\\]\nEach spatial constituent contributes \\(O(1)\\) by Proposition 2 and form monotonicity. This proves the energy estimate, which uses the count and flat increments, not the form cap.</p><p>After an addition, \\(A\\) contains \\(S_{p_*}\\), so\n\\[\nq_A(u)\\ge ce^{-F(A)}\n\\qquad(|u|\\le cR_{p_*}).\n\\]\nConvolving with \\(q_{S_p}\\) over these integers yields\n\\[\nq_{A+S_p}(0)\\ge ce^{-F(A)}R_{p_*}/R_p.\n\\]\nInitially use only \\(u=0\\), with \\(1\\le R_{p_*}\\le2\\). Taking negative logarithms proves the spatial increment.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Finite compression data from a separated spectrum — original Section 5.3.** Assume Proposition 3 has an update request at level \\(b\\).", "statement": "<p>**Finite compression data from a separated spectrum — original Section 5.3.**</p><p>Assume Proposition 3 has an update request at level \\(b\\). Keep at least half the offending collection with a common high child, and snap every retained integer to a nearest multiple \\(gx\\), where\n\\[\ng=\\max(1,\\lfloor\\sigma R_p/20\\rfloor).\n\\]\nLet \\(S\\subset\\mathbb Z\\) be the resulting indices. With \\(\\widetilde A\\) from Lemma 3.1, define\n\\[\nN_g=\\sum_{x\\in\\mathbb Z}q_{\\widetilde A}(gx),\n\\quad\nq(x)=q_{\\widetilde A}(gx)/N_g,\n\\quad\nd\\omega=K_{\\widetilde A}(y)\\,dy.\n\\]</p><p>The indices are distinct. For every \\(x\\in S\\), there is measurable \\(v_x\\), \\(|v_x|\\le1\\), such that\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}\\mathbb E_P[\n\\mathbf1_{\\mathrm{child}}v_x(Z)\\chi_{gx}(Y)]\n\\ge\\beta=b^2/100.\n\\]\nFor fixed sufficiently large \\(D_0,P_0\\), the mass function \\(q\\), measure \\(\\omega\\), grid \\(g\\), and set \\(S\\) satisfy Proposition 1's characteristic and sumset hypotheses. For every probability \\(\\nu\\) on \\(S\\),\n\\[\nE_T(\\nu)\n:=\n-\\log\\mathbb E_\\nu\nH_q\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^TX_i-\\sum_{i=1}^TX'_i\\right)\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n0\\le E_T(\\nu)\\le D_0d,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\ne^{-E_T(\\nu)}\n=\n\\int\\left|\\sum_x\\nu(x)\\chi_{gx}(y)\\right|^{2T}\nK_{\\widetilde A}(y)\\,dy.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A collision after snapping would violate separation. The snapping error is at most \\(\\sigma R_p/20\\), and is zero when \\(g=1\\).</p><p>Since \\(|f^u_{p'}|\\le M_{p'}\\),\n\\[\n\\int|f^u_{p'}|\\ge G^u_{p'}.\n\\]\nPolarization gives bias\n\\[\n\\frac{\\alpha}{2m}\\int|f^u_{p'}|\\ge b^2/4.\n\\]\nA constant phase rotation at the center of \\(p\\) makes the snapping loss at most \\(C\\sigma=o(b^2)\\), proving the stated bias.</p><p>The frequency-one spatial term makes every coefficient of \\(q_{\\widetilde A}\\) strictly positive. The grid identity and Gaussian comparison give\n\\[\nN_g=\\frac1g\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{g-1}e^{-\\widetilde A(\\ell/g)},\n\\qquad\n1/g\\le N_g\\le C/g.\n\\]\nHere \\(g\\le R_p\\), the summands are bounded by\n\\[\n\\exp(-cR_p^2\\|\\ell/g\\|^2),\n\\]\nand \\(R_p/g\\le C/\\sigma\\), including \\(g=1\\). Lemma 3.1 and the form cap imply\n\\[\n-\\log q(0)=F(\\widetilde A)-\\log g+O(1)\\le D_0d.\n\\]\nEvenness gives \\(q(x)/q(0)=\\int\\chi_{gx}\\,d\\omega\\).</p><p>For the sumset bound, the law of \\(gY\\) modulo one has density at most\n\\[\ne^{2d}(1+R_p/g).\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nI(Y;Z)\\le F(A)-\\log R_{p_*}+C.\n\\]\nTo see this, compare \\(K_A(z-Y)\\) with\n\\(K_{S_{p_*}}(z-\\operatorname{center}(p_*))\\). The average relative entropy is\n\\[\nF(A)-F(S_{p_*})-\\int AK_A\n+\\mathbb ES_{p_*}(\\theta+Y-\\operatorname{center}(p_*)).\n\\]\nThe last expectation is \\(O(1)\\) by the phase triangle inequality, the spatial energy bound, and \\(Y\\in p_*\\). Initially the same estimate is immediate.</p><p>Let \\(Q\\) consist of \\(Z\\) and the child bit. Then\n\\[\nD((gY,Q)\\|\\mathrm{uniform}\\otimes\\mathcal L(Q))\\le C(C_*)d.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\Lambda\\subset S\\) is dissociated, form the positive product\n\\[\n\\prod_{x\\in\\Lambda}\n\\left(1+c\\beta\\operatorname{Re}\n[\\mathbf1_{\\mathrm{child}}v_x(Z)\\chi_x(gY)]\\right).\n\\]\nFor small numerical \\(c\\), all factors lie in \\([1/2,3/2]\\). Under the reference law, dissociation makes its expectation exactly one. Relative entropy bounds below its true expected logarithm. The bias and\n\\(\\log(1+s)\\ge s-Cs^2\\) give at least \\(c'\\beta^2\\) per factor. Thus\n\\[\n|\\Lambda|\\le C(C_*)d/\\beta^2.\n\\]\nA maximal dissociated subset spans \\(S\\) by coefficients \\(0,\\pm1\\), so\n\\[\n|nS|\\le(2n+1)^{|\\Lambda|}.\n\\]\nAs \\(\\beta\\ge ch^{-32}\\), this gives the required fixed \\(D_0,P_0\\) sumset bound.</p><p>For the moment cap, restrict \\(Z\\) to the domain \\(\\Omega\\) within circle distance \\(h^{40}/R_{p_*}\\) of \\(p\\). Spatial variance discards probability \\(O(h^{-80})=o(\\beta)\\). Averaging the biases with \\(\\nu\\) and applying Jensen on a subprobability measure gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_P\\left[\n\\mathbf1_{\\mathrm{child}}\\mathbf1_{\\{Z\\in\\Omega\\}}\n\\left|\\sum_x\\nu(x)v_x(Z)\\chi_{gx}(Y)\\right|^{2T}\n\\right]\\ge(\\beta/2)^{2T}.\n\\]</p><p>For the upper bound, first discard the child indicator and majorize the nonnegative integrand using the spatial density bound \\(R_pe^{2d}\\) and\n\\[\n\\mathbf1_p(y)\\le\n\\exp(C-S_p(y-\\operatorname{center}(p))).\n\\]\nOnly then expand the even power. Proposition 2 bounds the shifted thermal Fourier coefficients by\n\\[\nCq_{A+S_p}(u)\n\\le Ce^{-F(A+S_p)}\\int\\chi_uK_{\\widetilde A}.\n\\]\nThe factors \\(v_x(z)\\) have modulus at most one. Averaging this envelope over the independent letters in the even-power expansion, and using\n\\(|\\Omega|\\le Ch^{40}/R_{p_*}\\), bounds the moment by\n\\[\nCR_pe^{2d+F(A)}\n\\frac{h^{40}}{R_{p_*}}\ne^{-F(A+S_p)}e^{-E_T(\\nu)}.\n\\]\nNow\n\\[\nF(A+S_p)\\ge\\log R_p-C,\\qquad\nF(A)\\le\\log R_{p_*}+C_*d,\n\\]\nand \\(T\\log(2/\\beta)=o(d)\\). Comparing the two moment bounds proves \\(E_T(\\nu)\\le D_0d\\). The argument applies the density bound only inside a nonnegative integral, not as a Fourier coefficient bound for \\(a\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Regular evidence — original Proposition 5.4.** Use the channels and updates of Proposition 3 in a finite procedure beginning with a type-zero main label and making at most \\(\\lfloor C_0h\\rfloor\\) successful main …", "statement": "<p>**Regular evidence — original Proposition 5.4.**</p><p>Use the channels and updates of Proposition 3 in a finite procedure beginning with a type-zero main label and making at most \\(\\lfloor C_0h\\rfloor\\) successful main additions. All discrete choices depend only on spatial prefixes and input memberships. Conditional on the input \\(Y\\) and its discrete parameter path, every label uses fresh independent error with its prescribed law.</p><p>At every success draw a new main label \\(V\\) of type \\(A^+\\). At a regular success also draw\n\\[\nN_h=\\lceil h^{120}\\rceil\n\\]\nfresh independent type-\\(A\\) clones \\(Z'\\). Include these draws even at a final success that breaches a stopping cap.</p><p>In the reference experiment, \\(Y\\) is initially uniform on the top interval, spatial bits observe \\(Y\\), and channels are the prescribed channels on the recorded branch. Membership outcomes instead are independent coins with the true-state probabilities \\(\\delta,1-\\delta\\). Conditional on a recorded past at interval \\(p\\), its posterior density is proportional to\n\\[\n\\mathbf1_p(y)\\exp\\left(-\\sum_jA_j(z_j-y)\\right),\n\\]\nwhere the sum includes past main labels and evidence clones.</p><p>Set \\(s_d=dh^{-100}\\). A past is admissible if some \\(y_0\\in p\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\sum_jA_j(z_j-y_0)\\le s_d.\n\\]</p><p>At an admissible regular attempt, conditional on its reference past and success coin, the observable event\n\\[\n\\text{for some fresh clone }Z':\n\\quad\nA(Z'-Z)\\le4s_d,\n\\quad\n\\left|\\sum_x\\nu(x)v_x(Z')\\chi_{gx}(V)\\right|\\ge\\beta/4\n\\]\nhas probability at most \\(e^{-t/8}\\), where \\(Z\\) is the old main label.</p><p>Under the true input law, all attempted pasts are admissible and this event occurs at every regular success simultaneously, except on a set of probability \\(o(h^{-20})\\). The estimates include the final cap-breaching success and are uniform in depth, cutoff, and deterministic choices.</p>", "proof": "<p>The observation count is at most \\(1+C_0h(1+N_h)\\), each old form is \\(\\preceq A\\), and hence\n\\[\n2\\sum_jA_j\\preceq h^{130}A\n\\]\nfor sufficiently large \\(d\\). At an admissible past, the phase triangle inequality gives\n\\[\n\\int_p\\exp\\left(-\\sum_jA_j(z_j-y)\\right)\\,dy\n\\ge c\\exp(-2s_d-F(\\widetilde A)).\n\\]\nIndeed, one direction from \\(y_0\\) contains a segment of length \\(|p|/2\\) inside \\(p\\). By symmetry and the spatial variance estimate, \\(K_{\\widetilde A}(y-y_0)\\) gives this segment positive numerical mass if \\(C_s\\) is large enough.</p><p>Fix a clone value \\(z'\\) in its consistency set and write \\(\\theta_+=V-Y\\). Expand the \\(2T\\)-moment of\n\\[\n\\left|\\sum_x\\nu(x)v_x(z')\\chi_{gx}(Y+\\theta_+)\\right|\n\\]\nin the posterior integral. The centered total form is\n\\[\nP=\\sum_jA_j+A+S_p,\n\\qquad\nA+S_p\\preceq P\\preceq\\widetilde A.\n\\]\nProposition 2 bounds the integral, before clone normalization and posterior division, by\n\\[\nCe^{-F(A+S_p)}e^{-E_T(\\nu)}\n\\le Ce^{-F(A+S_p)}e^{-t/4}.\n\\]\nThe consistency set has length at most \\(e^{4s_d-F(A)}\\), canceling clone normalization up to \\(e^{4s_d}\\). The \\(\\theta_+\\)-density integrates to one. Dividing by the posterior lower bound, applying the moment tail bound at \\(\\beta/4\\), and summing over clones gives\n\\[\nCN_h\\exp\\left(\n6s_d+F(\\widetilde A)-F(A+S_p)\n+2T\\log(4/\\beta)-t/4\n\\right)\n\\le e^{-t/8}.\n\\]\nLemma 3.1, \\(t\\ge\\varepsilon k_b\\), and \\(2T\\log(4/\\beta)=o(t)\\) absorb all positive terms. The reference success coin does not alter the posterior.</p><p>For truth, condition on \\(Y\\) and its complete discrete parameter path. The regular-success condition gives conditional mean modulus at least \\(\\beta/2\\). The empirical mean of the \\(N_h\\) clone polynomials at \\(Y\\) approximates this mean within \\(\\beta/16\\), except with probability\n\\[\nC/(\\beta^2N_h).\n\\]\nReplacing \\(Y\\) by \\(V\\), and removing the common phase \\(\\chi_{gx_0}(V-Y)\\), changes each polynomial by at most\n\\[\n\\sum_x\\nu(x)|1-\\chi_{g(x-x_0)}(V-Y)|.\n\\]\nIts expected value is at most\n\\[\n\\sqrt{2\\int BK_{A^+}}\\le C\\sqrt{t/\\lambda}=O(h^{-100}).\n\\]\nMarkov's inequality makes this at most \\(\\beta/16\\), except with probability \\(o(h^{-30})\\). The empirical-mean exception is \\(O(h^{-56})\\). Thus at least one clone has the required large polynomial value outside these errors.</p><p>Finally sum every observation's own-error energy along the capped true path. There are \\(O(h^{121})\\) observations, each with conditional expected energy\n\\[\nO(h(dh^{-400}+1)).\n\\]\nTheir total expected energy is\n\\[\nO(dh^{-278}+h^{122})=o(s_dh^{-30}).\n\\]\nExcept with probability \\(o(h^{-30})\\), the total is at most \\(s_d\\). Taking \\(y_0=Y\\) makes every past admissible. It also gives\n\\[\nA(Z'-Z)\\le2A(Z'-Y)+2A(Z-Y)\\le4s_d.\n\\]\nUnion over the \\(O(h)\\) regular successes proves the simultaneous assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**The finite dyadic procedure and live budgets — original Proposition 6.2.** Let \\(d\\) be sufficiently large, \\(h=\\log\\log d\\), and let \\(I\\subset\\mathbb R\\) be half-open with \\(1/2\\le|I|\\le1\\).", "statement": "<p>**The finite dyadic procedure and live budgets — original Proposition 6.2.**</p><p>Let \\(d\\) be sufficiently large, \\(h=\\log\\log d\\), and let \\(I\\subset\\mathbb R\\) be half-open with \\(1/2\\le|I|\\le1\\). Let \\(U\\) be normalized uniform probability on \\(I\\), and\n\\[\n0\\le\\rho\\le e^d,\\qquad\nm_{\\mathrm{top}}=\\mathbb E_I\\rho\\le2.\n\\]\nFix a finite dyadic tree and retain one additional output bit. At a depth-\\(j\\) interval \\(p\\), \\(r_{j+1}\\) is \\(+1\\) on its left child and \\(-1\\) on its right child. Every piece uses the common stop\n\\[\n\\tau(x)=\n\\min\\bigl(\\{0\\le j&lt;J:\\mathbb E_{p_j(x)}\\rho&gt;e^{4h}\\}\\cup\\{J\\}\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>Discard zero input. If \\(0&lt;m_{\\mathrm{top}}&lt;1/h\\), send the whole input to static handling. Otherwise use the true input law\n\\[\ndP=(\\rho/m_{\\mathrm{top}})\\,dU.\n\\]</p><p>A state at \\(p\\) carries a subdensity \\(a\\), obtained by restricting \\(\\rho\\) to its recorded memberships, and mean \\(m=\\mathbb E_pa\\). A live state also carries a thermal form \\(A\\), a successful-addition count \\(n\\), and regular charge\n\\[\nQ=\\sum_{\\text{regular successes}}t,\n\\]\nall initially zero and never reset.</p><p>For fixed caps \\(R_0,C_0,C_*\\), process each incoming state before its spatial jump as follows.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Transfer the whole state to static handling if\n   \\[\n   m&lt;e^{-d},\\qquad Q&gt;R_0d,\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad n\\ge\\lfloor C_0h\\rfloor.\n   \\]\n   A static piece undergoes no further membership splitting.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The initial live piece begins an epoch. Every successful addition begins a new epoch on its success piece unless it transfers immediately. An epoch has plateaus with fixed channel and normalization \\(\\alpha\\), initially the entry mean. Restart a plateau when an incoming mean exceeds \\(2\\alpha\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. At a live state with \\(e^{-d}\\le m\\le2\\alpha\\), apply all spectral tests of Proposition 3. An offending test splits\n   \\[\n   a=a\\mathbf1_E+a\\mathbf1_{E^c}.\n   \\]\n   The success piece receives \\(A^+\\), an increment of \\(n\\), and, for regular success, \\(t\\) added to \\(Q\\). Failure retains the old epoch, plateau, form, and charge.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Reprocess both pieces at the same interval until every surviving live piece is stabilized. Then take the spatial jump and proceed to children, subject to the common stop.</p><p>Static plateaus restart when their mean exceeds twice their normalization. All tests and choices are input-only and prefix-determined. Attach the type-zero initial main label and every success draw from Proposition 4, including terminal success draws.</p><p>At a jumping node, use the final post-processing survivor of each piece and its prospective restrictions to the two children. These jumping densities sum exactly to \\(\\rho|_p\\).</p><p>For a live plateau \\(e\\), let \\(p_e\\) be its root, \\(\\alpha_e\\) its entry mean, and \\(U_e=U(\\cdot\\mid p_e)\\). At a final live jumping state, set\n\\[\nM_\\pm(z)=\\mathbb E_{p^\\pm}[aK_A(z-Y)]/\\alpha_e,\n\\quad\nM=(M_++M_-)/2,\\quad D=(M_+-M_-)/2,\n\\]\n\\[\nJ_p=\\int |D|^2/M,\n\\]\nwith zero quotients interpreted as zero. Then \\(\\int M\\le2\\), \\(\\int M_\\pm\\le4\\), and \\(J_p\\le2\\).</p><p>There are absolute choices of the constants such that the procedure is finite, preserves the exact splitting identity, and, when \\(1/h\\le m_{\\mathrm{top}}\\le2\\), has the following budgets.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every requested update satisfies Proposition 3's hypotheses. On every input branch, including a final cap-crossing success,\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{\\mathrm{successes}}k_b+\n   \\sum_{\\mathrm{successes}}t+\n   \\sum_{\\mathrm{successes}}\n   \\bigl(F(A^+)-F(A+S_p)\\bigr)\\le Cd,\n   \\qquad n\\le Ch.\n   \\]\n   The count flag cannot cause transfer unless the charge flag has already been crossed at that success.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Let \\(B_{\\mathrm{mem}}\\) be total membership surprisal, and let \\(X_*\\) be the maximum \\(\\log^-m\\) through live termination, including transfer or common stopping but excluding later static descendants. Then\n   \\[\n   \\|B_{\\mathrm{mem}}\\|_{L^2(P)}+\\|X_*\\|_{L^2(P)}\\le Ch,\n   \\qquad\n   P(\\mathrm{transfer})\\le Ch^{-10}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The live plateau weights satisfy\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{e\\ \\mathrm{live}}U(p_e)\\alpha_e\\le Ch.\n   \\]\n   If \\(\\xi_p\\) is removed local mean divided by \\(\\alpha_e\\), including full removal at exit if desired, then\n   \\[\n   \\mathbb E_{U_e}\\sum_p\\xi_p\\le1.\n   \\]\n   Forward from a final post state \\(p_0\\), excluding completed removals, this is at most \\(m_{p_0}/\\alpha_e\\le2\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. The global main-label Fisher sum satisfies\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{\\text{live jumping pieces }p}U(p)\\alpha_eJ_p\\le Cd.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">5. In the unlabelled spatial-and-membership filtration, freeze the final piece at live termination and reveal any finite number of further spatial bits. The expected sum of spatial bit relative entropies to fair is \\(O(d)\\). If this extension stops at the common density cutoff, it is \\(O(h)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>**Caps and termination.**\nLet \\(C_1,C_2,\\varepsilon\\) be the numerical constants of Proposition 3, and take \\(R_0=1000\\). Telescoping the gap potential gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\mathrm{cheap}}k_b\\le2C_2\\sum_{\\mathrm{regular}}t+C,\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\mathrm{successes}}t\n\\le(1+2\\varepsilon C_2)\\sum_{\\mathrm{regular}}t+C.\n\\]\nRegular successes satisfy \\(\\varepsilon k_b\\le t\\). Thus, while \\(Q\\le R_0d\\), all success \\(k_b\\)'s sum to \\(C(R_0,\\varepsilon,C_2)d\\). Since each \\(k_b\\ge d/h\\), the success count is at most \\(C'h\\). Choose \\(C_0&gt;C'+2\\). A first count breach without a charge breach contradicts this prefix bound.</p><p>By Lemma 3.1 and Proposition 3,\n\\[\nF(A^+)-F(A)\n\\le\\log(R_p/R_{p_*})+C+C_1t.\n\\]\nSpatial scale ratios telescope along a branch. The preceding charge and count bounds imply\n\\[\nF(A)\\le\\log R_{p_*}+C_*d\n\\]\nbefore every update, for sufficiently large fixed \\(C_*\\). The flat-increment condition is provided by each update. Choose \\(D_0,P_0\\) from these caps, then \\(A_0\\), then the threshold for \\(d\\). A first charge-breaching success adds at most \\(D_0d\\) and transfers immediately, so its overshoot only changes absolute budget constants. Lemma 3.1's energy bound remains valid for its terminal draws.</p><p>At a fixed spatial node, only boundedly many successes can occur along a recurrence path. Each intervening failure multiplies mass by at most\n\\[\n1-c(b_0/8)^2.\n\\]\nSince \\(m\\le e^d\\), only finitely many consecutive failures occur before \\(m&lt;e^{-d}\\). The local binary recursion has uniformly finite depth and finite branching; the finite spatial tree therefore terminates. Frequencies and finite offending collections can be chosen by fixed enumeration, polarization signs are measurable, and membership probabilities are computed from their channel laws. Thus all pieces are measurable. Their partition identity follows from the successive membership restrictions.</p><p>**Spatial likelihood and membership cost.**\nAt a positive-mass state,\n\\[\nP(\\mathrm{state})=\\frac{U(p)m}{m_{\\mathrm{top}}},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal L(Y\\mid\\mathrm{state})=(a/m)\\mathcal L(U\\mid p).\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nL_{\\mathrm{sp}}=\\log(m/m_{\\mathrm{top}})+B_{\\mathrm{mem}}.\n\\]\nA membership outcome of probability \\(q\\) sends \\(m\\) to \\(qm\\) and adds \\(-\\log q\\), leaving \\(L_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\) unchanged. A spatial bit of true child probability \\(q\\) adds \\(\\log(2q)\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_P[e^{-L_{\\mathrm{sp,new}}}\\mid\\mathrm{past}]\n\\le e^{-L_{\\mathrm{sp,old}}}.\n\\]\nThis remains true under stopping or frozen-piece spatial extensions.</p><p>Success surprisal is at most \\(C(1+\\log(1/b))\\), and\n\\[\n1+\\log(1/b)\\le C(h/d)k_b.\n\\]\nIts path total is therefore \\(Ch\\). Between successes, accumulate failure surprisal \\(F\\); keep \\(e^F\\) unchanged at spatial steps, multiply it by \\((1-\\delta)^{-1}\\) on failure, and send it to zero on success. This is a nonnegative supermartingale. Its maximal inequality gives\n\\[\nP(F_{\\mathrm{final}}&gt;s\\mid\\mathrm{stretch\\ start})\\le e^{-s}.\n\\]\nThere are at most \\(Ch+1\\) stretches. Their conditional second moments are at most two; padding by zero stretches and applying the \\(L^2\\) triangle inequality gives\n\\[\n\\|B_{\\mathrm{mem}}\\|_2\\le Ch.\n\\]\nThe inverse spatial likelihood similarly gives a bounded \\(L^2\\) norm for \\(\\max(-L_{\\mathrm{sp}})_+\\). Since \\(m_{\\mathrm{top}}\\ge1/h\\),\n\\[\nX_*\\le\\log h+B_{\\mathrm{mem}}+\\max(-L_{\\mathrm{sp}})_+,\n\\]\nso \\(\\|X_*\\|_2\\le Ch\\).</p><p>Every regular attempt has success probability at least \\(ch^{-32}\\), while there are at most \\(Ch\\) successes. Summing conditional success probabilities gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_PN_{\\mathrm{att}}\\le Ch^{33}.\n\\]</p><p>**Likelihood evidence and rare transfer.**\nThe small-mass flag implies \\(X_*&gt;d\\), so has probability at most \\(Ch^2/d^2=o(h^{-10})\\). The count flag is covered by the charge flag.</p><p>Attach all observations and compare truth with Proposition 4's reference law \\(Q\\). For terminal observations \\(O\\), let \\(R=dP_O/dQ_O\\). On true histories,\n\\[\nR\\le\\exp(d+\\log h+B_{\\mathrm{mem}}).\n\\]\nFor each complete discrete history and its label values, truth integrates common channel weights over inputs compatible with both spatial bits and actual memberships, with prior density at most \\(e^d/m_{\\mathrm{top}}\\). Reference integrates over all spatially compatible inputs and contributes membership coin product \\(e^{-B_{\\mathrm{mem}}}\\). The true integration domain is a subset of the reference domain. This proves the likelihood bound on terminal branches of any length.</p><p>At an admissible regular attempt multiply a score by\n\\[\n\\frac{\\exp((t/16)\\mathbf1_{\\{\\mathrm{success}\\}\\cap E_{\\mathrm{ev}}})}\n{1+e^{-t/16}},\n\\]\nand otherwise leave it unchanged. Proposition 4 gives conditional reference mean at most one, because\n\\[\n1+(e^{t/16}-1)e^{-t/8}\\le1+e^{-t/16}.\n\\]\nThus the score \\(S\\) is a nonnegative reference supermartingale, \\(\\mathbb E_QS\\le1\\), and\n\\[\nP(S&gt;Re^d)\n=\\int_{\\{S&gt;Re^d\\}}R\\,dQ\n\\le e^{-d}\\mathbb E_QS\\le e^{-d}.\n\\]</p><p>True admissibility and evidence fail with probability \\(o(h^{-20})\\). Also\n\\[\nP(B_{\\mathrm{mem}}&gt;d)\\le Ch^2/d^2.\n\\]\nThe expected total logarithmic denominator cost is at most\n\\[\n\\mathbb E N_{\\mathrm{att}}\\,e^{-cd/h},\n\\]\nso, outside an \\(o(h^{-10})\\) exception, it is at most one. Outside all exceptions,\n\\[\nQ/16-1\\le\\log S\\le\\log R+d\n\\le2d+\\log h+B_{\\mathrm{mem}}\\le3d+\\log h.\n\\]\nThis excludes \\(Q&gt;1000d\\), proving the transfer bound.</p><p>**Plateau weights.**\nAt a plateau root,\n\\[\n\\sum_{e\\ \\mathrm{live}}U(p_e)\\alpha_e\n=\nm_{\\mathrm{top}}\\mathbb E_P(\\text{number of live plateau starts}).\n\\]\nEpoch starts number at most \\(Ch+1\\). To count upward resets without another factor \\(h\\), consider one epoch, from \\(\\sigma\\) to \\(\\tau\\), including its ending success. Let\n\\[\n\\Delta=L_{\\mathrm{sp}}(\\tau)-L_{\\mathrm{sp}}(\\sigma).\n\\]\nAt the \\(j\\)-th reset \\(\\rho_j\\),\n\\[\nL_{\\mathrm{sp}}(\\rho_j)\\ge L_{\\mathrm{sp}}(\\sigma)+j\\log2.\n\\]\nOptional sampling gives\n\\[\nP(L_{\\mathrm{sp}}(\\tau)&lt;L_{\\mathrm{sp}}(\\rho_j)-r\\mid\\mathcal F_{\\rho_j}^{\\mathrm{disc}})\n\\le e^{-r},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[(-\\Delta)_+\\mid\\mathcal F_\\sigma^{\\mathrm{disc}}]\\le1.\n\\]\nIf \\(N\\) is this epoch's reset count, summing reset indicators gives\n\\[\n(1-e^{-r})\\mathbb E[N\\mid\\mathcal F_\\sigma^{\\mathrm{disc}}]\n\\le\n\\frac{\\mathbb E[\\Delta\\mid\\mathcal F_\\sigma^{\\mathrm{disc}}]+r+1}{\\log2}.\n\\]\nSum over epochs and take \\(r=2\\). The \\(\\Delta\\)'s telescope because memberships leave \\(L_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\) unchanged. At termination,\n\\[\nm\\le2e^{4h},\n\\qquad\nL_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\le4h+\\log2+\\log h+B_{\\mathrm{mem}}.\n\\]\nThe expected total resets are \\(O(h)\\), proving the weight bound.</p><p>Removed portions of a fixed plateau are disjoint subsets of its initial input mass. Multiplying each local removed mean by its uniform reaching probability and summing counts each input at most once. Division by \\(\\alpha_e\\) proves both removal bounds.</p><p>**Main-label information and Fisher cost.**\nFor a finite remaining spatial bit string, the entropy chain rule says that revealing a bit consumes its conditional relative entropy to fair. An intervening observation increases expected remaining deficit by its information about the remaining string, at most its information about \\(Y\\). The initial deficit is at most \\(d+\\log h\\). Total membership information is at most \\(\\mathbb EB_{\\mathrm{mem}}=O(h)\\), after removing observed-label conditioning.</p><p>Retain main observations but not evidence clones. At a success, compare the new label \\(V\\) with\n\\[\nQ_Z(v)=\n\\frac{\\exp[-A(v-Z)-S_p(v-\\operatorname{center}(p))]}\n{\\int\\exp[-A(w-Z)-S_p(w-\\operatorname{center}(p))]\\,dw}.\n\\]\nThe centered Fourier envelope and relative entropy comparison give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(Y;V\\mid\\text{main history through success})\n\\le{}&amp;F(A^+)-F(A+S_p)\\\\\n&amp;+\\mathbb E[A(V-Z)+S_p(V-\\operatorname{center}(p))\n\\mid\\text{that history}].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAverage over main histories at the fixed discrete success state. Input-only selection preserves the independent old and new error laws conditional on \\(Y\\) and that state. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathbb EA(V-Z)\n\\le4\\int AK_A\\le Ch(dh^{-400}+1),\n\\]\nand the spatial energy is \\(O(1)\\). Summing over success-state probabilities costs\n\\[\nO(h^2(dh^{-400}+1))+O(h)=o(d)+O(h).\n\\]\nThe relative metric terms sum to \\(O(d)\\) pathwise. Total main-label information is therefore \\(O(d)\\), including terminal draws.</p><p>At a final live state, the current main-label density is \\((\\alpha_e/m_p)M\\), and its next-bit bias is \\(D/M\\). The expected squared bias is\n\\[\n(\\alpha_e/m_p)J_p.\n\\]\nMultiplying by state probability \\(U(p)m_p/m_{\\mathrm{top}}\\) gives\n\\[\nU(p)\\alpha_eJ_p/m_{\\mathrm{top}}.\n\\]\nOlder main observations only increase average squared conditional bias by Jensen. A bit of bias \\(b\\) has relative entropy to fair at least \\(b^2/2\\). Entropy accounting proves the global Fisher bound.</p><p>Finally ignore all labels. The conditional mean increment of \\(L_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\) at each spatial bit is exactly its relative entropy to fair. Thus expected total bit deficit is expected terminal \\(L_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\). For any frozen-piece finite extension,\n\\[\nL_{\\mathrm{sp,final}}\\le d+\\log h+B_{\\mathrm{mem}},\n\\]\nand for the common-cutoff extension,\n\\[\nL_{\\mathrm{sp,final}}\\le4h+\\log2+\\log h+B_{\\mathrm{mem}}.\n\\]\nThese give \\(O(d)\\) and \\(O(h)\\), respectively.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Capture on a plateau — original Proposition 7.2.** Use Proposition 5's finite procedure.", "statement": "<p>**Capture on a plateau — original Proposition 7.2.**</p><p>Use Proposition 5's finite procedure. Fix a positive live or static plateau \\(e\\), with root \\(p_e\\), normalization \\(\\alpha\\), and uniform output law \\(U_e\\). At a jumping post state \\(p\\), let \\(a\\) be its final survivor. Its prospective child densities are its restrictions before child processing, so\n\\[\nm_p/\\alpha\\le2,\\qquad \\mathbb E_{p'}a/\\alpha\\le4.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\xi_p\\) be local removed mean divided by \\(\\alpha\\), including full exit if desired, and set\n\\[\nd_1=d\\quad\\text{on live plateaus},\n\\qquad\nd_1=d+2+\\log^-\\alpha\\quad\\text{on static plateaus}.\n\\]</p><p>There are two criteria.</p><p class=\"li\">Main criterion: on live plateaus, dyadic \\(b_0\\le b\\le1\\), \\(\\kappa=2\\), and \\(u\\) is high if \\(\\max_{p'}G^u_{p'}\\ge b^2\\), using the fixed main label.</p><p class=\"li\">Scalar criterion: dyadic \\(0&lt;b\\le b_0\\) on live plateaus and \\(0&lt;b\\le1\\) on static plateaus, \\(\\kappa=1\\), and \\(u\\) is high if\n  \\[\n  \\max_{p'}|\\mathbb E_{p'}[a\\chi_u]/\\alpha|\\ge b.\n  \\]</p><p>Set\n\\[\n\\ell_b=\\left\\lceil20\\log_2\\frac{d_1+2}{b}\\right\\rceil,\n\\qquad\n\\delta_p=2^{-\\ell_b}|p|.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\operatorname{edge}(p,b)\\) be the points of \\(p\\) within \\(4\\delta_p\\) of an endpoint or midpoint, and put\n\\[\ne_{p,b}=\\mathbb E_p[a\\mathbf1_{\\operatorname{edge}(p,b)}]/\\alpha.\n\\]\nThe jump is bad if \\(e_{p,b}\\ge cb^\\kappa\\), for a sufficiently small fixed \\(c&gt;0\\).</p><p>For every criterion and allowed level, there is a cumulative finite ordered list \\(D\\), initially empty, with appends prescribed at post states, such that\n\\[\nu\\text{ high and }p\\text{ not bad}\n\\Longrightarrow\n|u-v||p|\\le\\operatorname{poly}(1+|D|,d_1,1/b)\n\\quad\\text{for some }v\\in[D],\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n[D]=\\left\\{\\sum_{n\\in D}\\eta_nn:\\eta_n\\in\\{0,\\pm1\\}\\right\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_{U_e}|D|_{\\mathrm{final}}\\le Cd_1b^{-C}.\n\\]\nThis construction is independent of later list caps.</p><p>The bad-edge charges have the following bounds.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Globally over live pieces, one scalar level selected predictably at each piece has weighted charge \\(O(d)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The charge summed over all main levels and live pieces is \\(O(\\operatorname{poly}(h)(h+\\log d))\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Locally, one selected scalar level has charge \\(O(d_1)\\) under \\(U_e\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Forward from a live post state, the expected number of future bad jumps at a fixed main level is at most \\(Cdb^{-2}\\).</p><p>For every deterministic dyadic cap \\(D_{\\max}\\ge1\\), stop the snapshot scheme when \\(|D|&gt;D_{\\max}\\). It has an expected polynomial number of snapshots in \\(D_{\\max},d_1,1/b\\). A snapshot stores at most\n\\[\n\\lceil e^{k_{b/2}}\\rceil\n\\]\nmain representatives, or\n\\[\n\\exp(C(d_1+\\log(1/b)))\n\\]\nscalar representatives. Births and lists are determined before the birth jump.</p><p>A high \\(u\\) is activated at \\(p\\) if some \\(v\\in[D]\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|u-v||p|\\le\\sigma\n\\quad\\text{in the main case},\n\\qquad\n|u-v||p|\\le c_8b^4\n\\quad\\text{in the scalar case}.\n\\]\nAt activation, a chosen snapshot representative \\(s\\) gives a decomposition fixed for the remainder of the plateau:\n\\[\nu=s+n+a',\n\\qquad\n\\Delta_A(n)\\le r_{\\mathrm{sep}},\n\\qquad\n|a'||p_{\\mathrm{act}}|\\le C\\sigma\n\\]\nin the main case, and\n\\[\nu=s+a',\n\\qquad\n|a'||p_{\\mathrm{act}}|\\le c_1b^4\n\\]\nin the scalar case, with \\(c_1\\) sufficiently small.</p><p>Before first activation, all non-bad high jumps for \\(u\\) lie in one block of at most\n\\[\nH_b=C\\log(CD_{\\max}d_1/b)+C\\log(1/\\sigma)\n\\]\nconsecutive depths, while the cap is unbreached. No construction uses sampled side or auxiliary values on an output path.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 6.2 constructs the cap-independent broad list and proves its capture and expected-size bounds. Lemma 6.3 supplies all edge charges. Lemma 6.4 constructs predictable snapshots, bounds their representative counts and expected births, and proves the continuing cover property. Lemma 6.5 gives activation and its single missed block. Lemma 6.1 supplies the local entropy and Fisher accounting used in these constructions.</p><p>All decisions use deterministic state measures, not realized side or auxiliary values. The removal bounds used throughout follow from disjoint removed input sets, as in Proposition 5.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Local entropy and Fisher budgets — original Lemma 7.1.** In Proposition 6's setting, let \\(P_e\\) be the input experiment obtained by normalizing the entry density on \\(p_e\\).", "statement": "<p>**Local entropy and Fisher budgets — original Lemma 7.1.**</p><p>In Proposition 6's setting, let \\(P_e\\) be the input experiment obtained by normalizing the entry density on \\(p_e\\). It follows actual memberships and may freeze the terminal piece and reveal further spatial bits after exit. A jumping post state has probability\n\\[\nP_e(\\text{post state at }p)=U_e(p)m_p/\\alpha.\n\\]</p><p>With or without the fixed main label, the expected sum of spatial bit relative entropies to fair is \\(O(d_1)\\), including every finite extension after exit. If predictable finite-valued observations have total expected conditional entropy cost at most \\(B\\), the bound is \\(Cd_1+B\\).</p><p>Let \\(M\\) be the joint post mass measure of the side information and finite observations, normalized by \\(\\alpha\\), and \\(D_{\\mathrm{jump}}\\) half the difference of prospective child measures. Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{U_e}\\sum_p\\int\\frac{|D_{\\mathrm{jump}}|^2}{M}\n\\le C(d_1+B).\n\\]</p><p>Forward from a live post state \\(p_0\\), restart with its post density and fixed main label, discarding earlier auxiliary observations. The same bounds hold with \\(d_1=d\\), an additional mass-conversion factor \\(m_{p_0}/\\alpha\\le2\\), and only future removals. Retaining earlier auxiliary information \\(W\\) instead adds \\(I(Y;W\\mid Z)\\) to the initial budget, or \\(I(Y;W)\\) without a side label.</p>", "proof": "<p>The initial density deficit is at most \\(d-\\log\\alpha\\le Cd_1\\). On a live plateau, \\(\\alpha\\ge e^{-d}\\). For its fixed main label, compare \\(K_A(z-Y)\\) with the fixed spatial density at the last addition interval \\(p_*\\):\n\\[\nI(Y;Z)\n\\le F(A)-F(S_{p_*})-\\mathbb EA(\\theta)\n+\\mathbb ES_{p_*}(Y+\\theta-\\operatorname{center}(p_*))\n\\le Cd.\n\\]\nThe last energy is \\(O(1)\\) by spatial variance, form monotonicity, the phase triangle inequality, and \\(p_e\\subset p_*\\). The initial dummy label has no information. This comparison holds for every input law supported on \\(p_e\\), including later post laws.</p><p>Within one live plateau, at most one success occurs, terminal for that plateau. Its surprisal is \\(O(1+\\log h)\\); total failure surprisal has an exponential tail by Proposition 5's failure-stretch argument. Thus expected membership entropy is \\(O(1+\\log h)\\). Static plateaus have none. Forward from a live post state, \\(m_{p_0}\\ge e^{-d}\\), so the initial density deficit is at most \\(2d\\).</p><p>Apply the finite-string entropy chain rule exactly as in Proposition 5. Bits consume relative entropy; observations add at most their conditional information, bounded by Shannon entropy when finite-valued. Membership entropy can be bounded after removing side and auxiliary conditioning. The remaining deficit is nonnegative at stopping.</p><p>At \\(p\\), the observations have density \\((\\alpha/m_p)M\\) under truth and next-bit bias \\(D_{\\mathrm{jump}}/M\\). The squared bias averaged over observations is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\alpha}{m_p}\\int\\frac{|D_{\\mathrm{jump}}|^2}{M}.\n\\]\nMultiplication by the state probability cancels \\(m_p/\\alpha\\). Relative entropy to fair is at least half squared bias, and further past conditioning only increases average squared conditional bias. This proves the Fisher bound. Restarting leaves the factor \\(m_{p_0}/\\alpha\\). Retaining \\(W\\) adds precisely the stated initial information.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Broad capture by trimmed masks and polynomial products — original Section 7.3.** In Proposition 6's setting, a cumulative list \\(D\\) can be constructed so that every non-bad high integer is captured by \\([D]\\) within …", "statement": "<p>**Broad capture by trimmed masks and polynomial products — original Section 7.3.**</p><p>In Proposition 6's setting, a cumulative list \\(D\\) can be constructed so that every non-bad high integer is captured by \\([D]\\) within the polynomial spatial error stated there, and\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{P_e}(\\text{number of appends})\\le Cd_1b^{-2\\kappa},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_{U_e}|D|_{\\mathrm{final}}\\le Cd_1b^{-3\\kappa}.\n\\]\nAt every append state, \\(m_p/\\alpha\\ge b^\\kappa/2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a high \\(u\\), polarize on one child:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}\\frac1\\alpha\n\\mathbb E_p[a\\mathbf1_{p'}v(Z)\\chi_u(Y)]\n\\ge b^\\kappa/2,\n\\qquad |v|\\le1.\n\\]\nIn the scalar case \\(v\\) is constant. For the main criterion this follows from\n\\(\\int|f|\\ge\\int|f|^2/M\\). The displayed bias implies the mass lower bound.</p><p>Let \\(n=|D|\\). Trim a distance \\(\\delta_p\\) from the child endpoints and convolve with the positive normalized kernel proportional to\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{\\sin(\\pi Nx)}{N\\sin(\\pi x)}\\right)^{2s},\n\\quad\nN=\\lceil C2^{\\ell_b}/|p|\\rceil,\n\\quad\ns=\\lceil C(1+n+\\log(1/b))\\rceil.\n\\]\nThe resulting real trigonometric polynomial \\(P_p\\) satisfies \\(0\\le P_p\\le1\\),\n\\[\n\\deg P_p\\le\n\\frac{C2^{\\ell_b}(1+n+\\log(1/b))}{|p|},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathbb T\\setminus p}P_p\n\\le |p|\\exp[-C'(1+n+\\log(1/b))].\n\\]\nFor completeness, the unnormalized kernel integral is at least \\(c/(N\\sqrt s)\\), by its values within \\(c/(N\\sqrt s)\\) of zero. Using \\(|\\sin\\pi x|\\ge2\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\mathbb Z)\\), its tail beyond \\(\\delta_p\\) is at most \\(Ce^{-c's}/N\\), hence normalized tail at most \\(C\\sqrt s\\,e^{-c's}\\). Thus outside the edge \\(P_p\\) approximates \\(\\mathbf1_{p'}\\) with that error. The edge costs at most \\(e_{p,b}\\). At a non-bad jump, replacing the indicator preserves bias at least \\(c_2b^\\kappa\\).</p><p>For each fixed side value \\(z\\), maintain a full-circle polynomial product \\(Q_z\\), initially one. Its restriction normalized on the current interval is the reference input density. After \\(n\\) appends,\n\\[\n2^{-n}\\le Q_z\\le2^n,\n\\qquad\n\\int_pQ_z\\ge2^{-n}|p|.\n\\]\nIf a non-bad high \\(u\\) is outside \\([D]\\), enlarged by all accumulated mask bandwidths and the prospective bandwidth, append \\(u\\) and multiply \\(Q_z\\) by\n\\[\n1+\\eta b^\\kappa\\operatorname{Re}[v(z)\\chi_uP_p].\n\\]\nThe full-circle integral of the new linear term against \\(Q_z\\) vanishes by Fourier support. Restriction to \\(p\\) changes its normalized integral by at most\n\\[\n4^n\\exp[-C'(1+n+\\log(1/b))].\n\\]\nChoose \\(C'\\) to make this a sufficiently small multiple of \\(b^\\kappa\\).</p><p>Track relative entropy of truth conditional on the side value to this reference, averaged over the current side law. Dividing the normalized bias by \\(m_p/\\alpha\\le2\\), and using \\(\\log(1+x)\\ge x-Cx^2\\), shows that each append consumes at least \\(c_3b^{2\\kappa}\\) entropy.</p><p>If \\(H\\) is the discrete state history, set\n\\[\nB(H)=\\mathbb E_{Z\\mid H}\nD(\\mathcal L(Y\\mid H,Z)\\|Q_{H,Z}).\n\\]\nA spatial bit restricts the reference to the selected child. The entropy chain rule gives\n\\[\n\\sum_xP(x\\mid H)B(H,x)\n=\nB(H)-\\mathbb E_{Z\\mid H}\nD(\\mathcal L(X\\mid H,Z)\\|\\mathcal L_{Q_{H,Z}}(X))\n\\le B(H).\n\\]\nA membership observation increases average entropy by\n\\(I(Y;C\\mid H,Z)\\le H(C\\mid H)\\). Exited branches have nonnegative entropy and may be discarded. Lemma 6.1 bounds the initial budget and memberships by \\(Cd_1\\). This proves the \\(P_e\\)-append bound. The state conversion and append mass lower bound give the \\(U_e\\)-bound.</p><p>At a fixed state the entropy is finite and drops strictly at each append, so the append loop is finite. Once no append is possible, accumulated bandwidth multiplied by \\(|p|\\) is at most\n\\[\nC2^{\\ell_b}(n+1)(n+1+\\log(1/b)).\n\\]\nFor example \\(C(d_1+2)^{20}b^{-21}(n+1)^2\\) is an admissible polynomial. Choices depend on laws, not sampled side values.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Edge charges — original Lemma 7.3.** With Proposition 6's bad-jump convention:", "statement": "<p>**Edge charges — original Lemma 7.3.**</p><p>With Proposition 6's bad-jump convention:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Over live jumping pieces, one scalar level selected before each next output bit has\n   \\[\n   \\sum_pU(p)\\alpha_e e_{p,b}\\mathbf1_{\\mathrm{bad}}\\le Cd.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Summing over all main levels gives at most\n   \\[\n   C\\operatorname{poly}(h)(h+\\log d).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Within one plateau, the corresponding expected one-scalar-level charge is at most \\(Cd_1\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Forward from a live post state, the expected number of future bad jumps at main level \\(b\\) is at most \\(Cdb^{-2}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the global unlabelled input experiment for the first two assertions and the local experiment of Lemma 6.1 for the last two. Freeze the terminal piece at exit and extend bits as needed, so all windows use one predictable spatial entropy sequence.</p><p>At a bad post state, true edge probability is\n\\[\nq=\\alpha e_{p,b}/m_p\\ge cb^\\kappa/2.\n\\]\nCompare the next \\(\\ell=\\ell_b\\) bits with an experiment having fair spatial bits and the same state-conditional membership probabilities. Membership likelihood factors cancel, and its spatial path is uniform. The edge is determined by these bits, with reference probability at most \\(16\\,2^{-\\ell}\\).</p><p>If \\(L\\) is the segment likelihood ratio, then\n\\[\nP_{\\mathrm{true}}(\\operatorname{edge}\\cap\\{L&lt;2^{\\ell/2}\\}\\mid\\mathrm{post})\n\\le16\\,2^{-\\ell/2}\\le q/2.\n\\]\nMark a starting depth when the edge occurs and \\(\\log L\\ge(\\ell/2)\\log2\\). Its conditional probability at a bad state is at least \\(q/2\\).</p><p>Let \\(s_j\\) be predictable bit relative entropy to fair and \\(\\theta_j\\) its bias. Then\n\\[\ns_j\\ge\\theta_j^2/2,\n\\qquad\n[\\log(1\\pm\\theta_j)]_+\\le|\\theta_j|\\le\\sqrt{2s_j}.\n\\]\nOn a marked window, Cauchy–Schwarz implies\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\text{ in window}}s_j\\ge c_4\\ell.\n\\]\nThere is at most one final jumping piece at each depth along a true input path. From a finite family of marked windows select disjoint windows whose triples cover their union, for example by repeatedly taking a longest remaining window. The number of starting indices is at most a constant times the selected lengths, hence at most \\(C\\sum_js_j\\). This works for variable window lengths.</p><p>The global \\(O(d)\\) spatial entropy budget from Proposition 5 therefore gives\n\\[\n\\sum_pU(p)\\alpha_ee_{p,b}\\mathbf1_{\\mathrm{bad}}\n=\nm_{\\mathrm{top}}\\mathbb E_P\\sum_pq\\mathbf1_{\\mathrm{bad}}\n\\le2m_{\\mathrm{top}}\\mathbb E_P(\\text{number of marks})\n\\le Cd.\n\\]\nNonnegativity permits passage from finite selections to the full sum.</p><p>For a fixed main level, \\(\\ell_b\\) is constant on live paths. Windows wholly before the common cutoff use the \\(O(h)\\) truncated budget; at most \\(\\ell_b\\) starting indices straddle it. Continuing frozen pieces after an earlier transfer preserves this budget. Thus a fixed main level costs \\(O(h+\\ell_b)\\). There are \\(O(\\log h)\\) levels and\n\\[\n\\ell_b=O(\\log d+\\log(1/b_0)),\n\\]\nproving the second assertion.</p><p>The local argument uses Lemma 6.1 and gives \\(Cd_1\\). Forward from a live post state, the mass conversion contributes at most two and the entropy budget is \\(Cd\\). Dividing its edge charge by the badness threshold \\(cb^2\\) gives the final assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Snapshot covers and birth count — original Lemma 7.4.** Fix a deterministic dyadic \\(D_{\\max}\\ge1\\).", "statement": "<p>**Snapshot covers and birth count — original Lemma 7.4.**</p><p>Fix a deterministic dyadic \\(D_{\\max}\\ge1\\). Stop the snapshot scheme when \\(|D|&gt;D_{\\max}\\). Round each \\(\\chi_n(Y)\\), \\(n\\in D\\), using a fixed partition into \\(O(D_{\\max}/b^6)\\) arcs, with error at most \\(c_5b^6/D_{\\max}\\). Track the joint finite mass measure of rounded coordinates and side information, divided by \\(\\alpha\\), without probability renormalization.</p><p>After the broad-list step at each jumping post node, refresh the snapshot if it is the first, if a coordinate was appended, or if some prospective child pre measure differs in full variation norm by more than \\(c_6b^6\\) from the stored post measure. Store the current post measure.</p><p>At a birth interval \\(q\\), take a maximal separated collection from \\([D]\\) high at threshold \\(b/2\\). Main separation excludes differences in\n\\[\nB_A(r_{\\mathrm{sep}})+\\{j:|j||q|\\le\\sigma\\}.\n\\]\nScalar separation requires difference magnitude greater than \\(c_7b^4/|q|\\).</p><p>For sufficiently small \\(c_5,c_6,c_7\\), the list sizes are those in Proposition 6. While current, a snapshot covers every signed sum high at \\(0.9b\\), using its birth neighborhoods. Its expected number of births satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{U_e}N_{\\mathrm{snap}}\n\\le\n1+D_{\\max}+Cb^{-6}\n+Cb^{-12}\\bigl(d_1+D_{\\max}\\log(CD_{\\max}/b)\\bigr).\n\\]\nEvery birth occurs before its node's next bit; representatives remain usable on the same plateau after replacement.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a stabilized live node, a separated high collection of size \\(\\lceil e^{k_{b/2}}\\rceil\\) would request an update at computation level \\(b/2\\). This proves the main list bound and cover.</p><p>For scalar lists, Parseval on a prospective child gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{u\\in\\mathbb Z}|f^u_{p'}|^2\n=\n\\frac1{\\alpha^2|p'|}\\int_{p'}a^2\n\\le\\frac{Ce^d}{\\alpha|p|}.\n\\]\nAfter a phase rotation, moments vary by at most \\(C|u-u'||p|\\). Around each separated representative choose an integer neighborhood of radius \\(c_7b^4/(3|p|)\\). The neighborhoods are disjoint and contain at least \\(cb^4/|p|\\) integers, including when this bound is less than one. On one child their moments remain at least \\(cb\\). Summing over both children bounds the number by\n\\[\nCe^d/(\\alpha b^6)\\le\\exp(C(d_1+\\log(1/b))).\n\\]</p><p>Every signed-sum phase is approximated by a product of rounded phases with error at most \\(c_5b^6\\). Scalar moments change by \\(O((c_5+c_6)b^6)\\) between a prospective child and the stored post law. For main moments use\n\\[\n\\int\\frac{|f|^2}{M}\n=\n\\sup_{|\\gamma(z)|\\le1}\n\\int\\bigl(2\\operatorname{Re}(\\gamma(z)\\chi_v)-|\\gamma(z)|^2\\bigr)\\,d\\mu.\n\\]\nThe tests are bounded by three; phase rounding and variation distance give the same transfer error. A child high at \\(0.9b\\) makes the stored post law high above \\(b/2\\). Scalar convexity and convexity of \\(|f|^2/M\\) ensure some stored prospective child is high at \\(b/2\\), giving the cover.</p><p>Each rounded coordinate is observed only at append, at entropy cost at most \\(C\\log(CD_{\\max}/b)\\). Lemma 6.1 gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{U_e}\\sum_p\\int\\frac{|D_{\\mathrm{jump}}|^2}{M}\n\\le Cd_1+CD_{\\max}\\log(CD_{\\max}/b).\n\\]\nForgetting coordinates decreases Fisher cost by conditional Jensen.</p><p>For birth counting, start a comparison interval at each snapshot's post measure \\(M_*\\), in its fixed coordinates. Appends or cap breach end the old comparison before that node's jump. A distance trigger without append includes its trigger bit and ends at the selected child pre measure; the new comparison starts at the trigger node's post measure before that same bit. Thus a bit belongs to at most two comparisons.</p><p>Retain every removed positive measure \\(k\\), and write \\(K\\) for their accumulated sum. Then\n\\[\nN=M+K\n\\]\nis a positive measure martingale under uniform output bits, starting at \\(M_*\\). At a bit it takes values \\(N\\pm D_{\\mathrm{jump}}\\), with \\(|D_{\\mathrm{jump}}|\\le M\\le N\\); removals leave it unchanged. All later measures are absolutely continuous with respect to \\(M_*\\).</p><p>Use generalized relative entropy\n\\[\n\\mathcal E(N\\mid M_*)\n=\n\\int(n\\log n-n+1)\\,dM_*,\n\\qquad n=dN/dM_*.\n\\]\nAt a bit its mean increment is at most\n\\[\nC\\int|D_{\\mathrm{jump}}|^2/N\n\\le C\\int|D_{\\mathrm{jump}}|^2/M.\n\\]\nBounded stopping bounds terminal expected entropy by the comparison's Fisher sum.</p><p>At a distance trigger, the selected child is farther than \\(c_6b^6\\) from \\(M_*\\) with probability at least one half. Then either \\(\\|K\\|\\ge c_6b^6/2\\), or\n\\(\\|N-M_*\\|_{\\mathrm{var}}&gt;c_6b^6/2\\). In the latter case with small \\(K\\), \\(N\\) has mass at most \\(4+c_6b^6/2\\) and \\(M_*\\) mass at most two. The inequality\n\\[\n\\frac{(x-1)^2}{1+x}\\le C(x\\log x-x+1)\n\\]\nand Cauchy–Schwarz imply\n\\[\n\\|N-M_*\\|_{\\mathrm{var}}^2\n\\le C(N(\\Omega)+M_*(\\Omega))\\mathcal E(N\\mid M_*).\n\\]\nThus that event forces entropy at least \\(cb^{12}\\).</p><p>Conditionally at the comparison start, trigger probability is therefore at most\n\\[\nC\\mathbb E\\left[\nb^{-6}\\sum_{\\mathrm{interval}}\\xi_p\n+\nb^{-12}\\sum_{\\mathrm{interval}}\n\\int|D_{\\mathrm{jump}}|^2/M\n\\,\\middle|\\,\\mathrm{start}\n\\right].\n\\]\nSum over starts. Bits and removals have bounded overlap; expected removal sum is at most one. Initialization costs one and append replacements at most \\(D_{\\max}\\). This proves the birth count. Replacing a snapshot does not re-observe coordinates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Activation and localization before activation — original Lemma 7.5.** In the capped scheme of Proposition 6 and Lemma 6.4, activation gives the fixed representative decompositions stated in Proposition 6.", "statement": "<p>**Activation and localization before activation — original Lemma 7.5.**</p><p>In the capped scheme of Proposition 6 and Lemma 6.4, activation gives the fixed representative decompositions stated in Proposition 6. All non-bad high jumps strictly before first activation lie in one block of at most\n\\[\nC\\log(CD_{\\max}d_1/b)+C\\log(1/\\sigma)\n\\]\ndepths.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(v\\in[D]\\) satisfies the activation tolerance, freeze \\(\\chi_{u-v}\\) at a point of \\(p\\). The normalized scalar moment error on a child is at most \\(C|u-v||p|\\). In the main case conditional Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\left|\\sqrt{G^u_{p'}}-\\sqrt{G^v_{p'}}\\right|\n\\le\n\\left(\n\\alpha^{-1}\\mathbb E_{p'}[\na|\\chi_{u-v}(Y)-\\chi_{u-v}(y_p)|^2]\n\\right)^{1/2}\n\\le C|u-v||p|.\n\\]\nThus \\(v\\) is high at \\(0.9b\\). Lemma 6.4 covers it by a stored representative \\(s\\). If the snapshot was born at \\(q\\supset p\\), the main cover gives\n\\[\nv-s=n+j,\\qquad\\Delta_A(n)\\le r_{\\mathrm{sep}},\n\\qquad |j||q|\\le\\sigma.\n\\]\nSet \\(a'=u-v+j\\). Then \\(|a'||p|\\le2\\sigma\\). The fixed plateau channel preserves the metric bound. The scalar cover instead gives\n\\[\n|v-s||q|\\le c_7b^4,\n\\]\nand hence the desired small scalar discrepancy. Fix the decomposition at activation.</p><p>If \\(p_0\\) is the first preactivation non-bad high jump, broad capture gives some available signed sum \\(v\\) with\n\\[\n|u-v||p_0|\\le P(D_{\\max},d_1,1/b)\n\\]\nfor a fixed polynomial \\(P\\). This \\(v\\) remains available as the list grows. After \\(r\\) depths the left side is at most \\(2^{-r}P\\). For\n\\[\nr\\ge C\\log(CD_{\\max}d_1/b)+C\\log(1/\\sigma),\n\\]\nit is within the relevant activation tolerance. Every later high jump activates, whether bad or not. If no \\(p_0\\) exists, there is no missed non-bad high jump.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Simultaneous plateau transform estimates — original Lemma 8.7, incorporating the interval conclusions of original Propositions 8.2 and 8.6.** Fix a plateau from Proposition 6, with normalization \\(\\alpha\\) and uniform …", "statement": "<p>**Simultaneous plateau transform estimates — original Lemma 8.7, incorporating the interval conclusions of original Propositions 8.2 and 8.6.**</p><p>Fix a plateau from Proposition 6, with normalization \\(\\alpha\\) and uniform output law \\(U_e\\). Let \\(\\mathcal F_j\\) contain the spatial prefix through depth \\(j\\) and its deterministic processing data. At a jumping post state the two prospective pre-child states and the retention decision are \\(\\mathcal F_j\\)-measurable. The sign \\(r_{j+1}\\) is conditionally fair. Multipliers satisfy\n\\[\n|\\epsilon_j|\\le1,\\qquad\\epsilon_j\\text{ is }\\mathcal F_{j+1}\\text{-measurable},\n\\]\nand are common to all label coordinates. Coefficients formed at depth \\(j\\) are emitted with \\(r_{j+2}\\). Put\n\\[\nK_e=\\sum_p\\xi_p.\n\\]</p><p>For every allowed scalar level \\(b\\), there are nonnegative, frequency-independent path parameters \\(t_b,H_b\\) such that every snapshot representative \\(s\\) in the selected capped scheme satisfies, for every contiguous interval \\(\\mathcal I\\) after its birth,\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal I}\n\\eta_pu_p\\epsilon_{j(p)}r_{j(p)+2}\n\\right|\n\\le Cb^{1/4}(1+K_e+t_b),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nf_p=\\mathbb E_p[a\\chi_s]/\\alpha,\\quad\nu_p=(f_{p,+}-f_{p,-})/2,\\quad\n\\eta_p=\\mathbf1_{\\{\\max(|f_{p,+}|,|f_{p,-}|)\\le C_3b\\}}.\n\\]\nMoreover\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{U_e}t_b\\le C(d_1+\\log(1/b)).\n\\]</p><p>For a main level \\(b\\in[b_0,1]\\), a fixed representative \\(s\\) has label-valued coefficients\n\\[\na_p(z)=\n\\mathbb E_p[a(Y)\\chi_s(Y)r_{j(p)+1}(Y)K_A(z-Y)]/\\alpha.\n\\]\nIf \\(b&lt;1\\), a jump is exceptional when it is bad at main level \\(2b\\), and\n\\[\ne'_p=Cb_0^{-2}e_{p,2b}\\mathbf1_{\\mathrm{exceptional}};\n\\]\ntake \\(e'_p=0\\) for \\(b=1\\). Put \\(E_{e,b}=\\sum_pe'_p\\).</p><p>A jump is inner-valid for \\(s,b\\) if exceptional or\n\\[\n\\max(G^s_+,G^s_-)\\le C_4b^2,\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_4\\) is a fixed sufficiently large numerical constant. At \\(b=1\\), every jump is inner-valid.</p><p>For every contiguous inner-valid interval \\(\\mathcal I\\) after any snapshot representative's birth,\n\\[\n\\int\\left|\n\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal I}a_p(z)\\epsilon_{j(p)}r_{j(p)+2}\n\\right|\\,dz\n\\le\nC\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal I}J_p\n+Cb^{-1/2}(1+K_e+E_{e,b})\n+Cb^{1/2}t_b,\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{U_e}t_b\n\\le Ck_{b/4}+C\\log(Cd/b).\n\\]\nThe coefficient of the Fisher sum is absolute.</p><p>For either criterion, activation has missed-block length \\(H_b\\), with\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{U_e}H_b\n\\le C\\log(Cd_1/b)+C\\log(1/\\sigma).\n\\]\nThese assertions hold almost surely simultaneously over all representatives, deterministic dyadic list caps before pathwise cap selection, and countably many permitted levels.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the simultaneous-representative and random-cap argument of original Lemma 8.7.</p><p>First fix a deterministic cap \\(D_{\\max}=2^m\\). The broad list is cap-independent, and the capped scheme births snapshots only before breach; estimates for already born representatives continue to plateau end. Lemma 6.4 bounds expected births by a polynomial in \\(D_{\\max},d_1,1/b\\). Each birth has at most\n\\[\nR_b^{\\mathrm{main}}=\\lceil e^{k_{b/2}}\\rceil,\n\\qquad\nR_b^{\\mathrm{sc}}=\\exp(C(d_1+\\log(1/b)))\n\\]\nrepresentatives. Lemma 7.6 contributes another polynomial expected origin count for each main representative. Since \\(b_0^{-1}\\le2h^{16}\\le d\\) for large \\(d\\), that factor is a fixed power of \\(d\\).</p><p>Enumerate predictable origins by finite tree addresses and representative indices. If \\(B_o\\) is failure with deterministic parameter \\(t\\) at a reached origin,\n\\[\nP(B_o)\\le Ce^{-t}P(o\\text{ reached}).\n\\]\nSumming uses expected origin count, not a deterministic count. No union over the signed span is taken.</p><p>Choose baseline parameters\n\\[\nT_b^{\\mathrm{main}}=Ck_{b/4}+C\\log(Cd/b),\n\\qquad\nT_b^{\\mathrm{sc}}=C(d_1+\\log(1/b)).\n\\]\nThey absorb representative cardinalities and all polynomial logarithms except the cap. Use\n\\[\nt=T_b+C_{\\mathrm{cap}}\\log(CD_{\\max})+z.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large fixed \\(C_{\\mathrm{cap}}\\), failure at cap \\(2^m\\) is at most \\(C2^{-2m}e^{-z}\\). Union over caps gives \\(Ce^{-z}\\).</p><p>The success events are increasing in \\(z\\). Let \\(Z_b\\) be the least nonnegative integer making all capped schemes succeed. Then\n\\[\nP(Z_b&gt;n)\\le Ce^{-n},\\qquad\\mathbb EZ_b\\le C,\n\\]\nand \\(Z_b&lt;\\infty\\) almost surely.</p><p>Only now choose the least dyadic \\(\\widehat D\\ge1+|D|_{\\mathrm{final}}\\), so\n\\[\n\\widehat D&lt;2(1+|D|_{\\mathrm{final}}).\n\\]\nIts scheme never breaches on this path. Set\n\\[\nt_b=T_b+C_{\\mathrm{cap}}\\log(C\\widehat D)+Z_b.\n\\]\nBroad capture and Jensen give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\log(C\\widehat D)\n\\le C+\\log(C(1+\\mathbb E|D|_{\\mathrm{final}}))\n\\le C\\log(Cd_1/b).\n\\]\nThis proves the stated expected \\(t_b\\) bounds. Lemma 6.5 with the same cap gives\n\\[\nH_b=C\\log(C\\widehat Dd_1/b)+C\\log(1/\\sigma)\n\\]\nand the required expectation. On static plateaus, \\(\\log(1/\\sigma)=40\\log h\\le Cd_1\\).</p><p>For each level the success event has probability one. Intersect over countably many levels. The resulting parameters depend on the plateau path and level, not on the actual integer frequency later estimated.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Predictable signs — original Lemma 8.1.** Let \\(r_{i+1}\\) be fair signs in a filtration \\((\\mathcal F_i)\\), and let \\(c_i\\) be real and \\(\\mathcal F_i\\)-measurable.", "statement": "<p>**Predictable signs — original Lemma 8.1.**</p><p>Let \\(r_{i+1}\\) be fair signs in a filtration \\((\\mathcal F_i)\\), and let \\(c_i\\) be real and \\(\\mathcal F_i\\)-measurable. For every \\(\\theta&gt;0\\) and \\(t\\ge0\\), outside probability \\(e^{-t}\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{i&lt;n}c_ir_{i+1}\n\\le\\frac\\theta2\\sum_{i&lt;n}c_i^2+\\frac t\\theta\n\\]\nfor every prefix \\(n\\).</p><p>The same holds conditionally from a stopping-time origin and with predictable stopping or masking. For complex sums, applying both signs to both real components gives total failure probability at most \\(4e^{-t}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The inequality \\(\\cosh x\\le e^{x^2/2}\\) makes\n\\[\n\\exp\\left(\n\\theta\\sum_{i&lt;n}c_ir_{i+1}\n-\\frac{\\theta^2}2\\sum_{i&lt;n}c_i^2\n\\right)\n\\]\na nonnegative supermartingale starting at one. Stop at its first crossing of \\(e^t\\). Finite depth makes optional sampling immediate. Conditional initialization proves the stopping-origin form, and predictable masks are absorbed into \\(c_i\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Scalar plateau paths — original Proposition 8.2.** For one scalar representative \\(s\\) born at a predictable snapshot, use \\(f_p,u_p,\\eta_p\\) from Proposition 7.", "statement": "<p>**Scalar plateau paths — original Proposition 8.2.**</p><p>For one scalar representative \\(s\\) born at a predictable snapshot, use \\(f_p,u_p,\\eta_p\\) from Proposition 7. For every \\(t\\ge0\\), with conditional failure probability at most \\(Ce^{-t}\\) at birth, every contiguous interval \\(\\mathcal I\\) of subsequent plateau jumps satisfies\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal I}\n\\eta_pu_p\\epsilon_{j(p)}r_{j(p)+2}\n\\right|\n\\le Cb^{1/4}(1+K_e+t).\n\\]\nConstants are independent of depth, \\(s,b\\), and may depend on \\(C_3\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Identify complex moments with \\(\\mathbb R^2\\) and put\n\\[\n\\psi(x)=b^{1/2}\\left((|x|^2+b^2)^{3/4}-b^{3/2}\\right).\n\\]\nOn \\(|x|\\le4\\),\n\\[\n0\\le\\psi(x)\\le Cb^{1/2},\\qquad|\\nabla\\psi(x)|\\le Cb^{1/2},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\nabla^2\\psi(x)\n=\n\\frac32b^{1/2}(|x|^2+b^2)^{-5/4}\n\\left((|x|^2+b^2)\\operatorname{Id}-\\tfrac12xx^{\\mathsf T}\\right).\n\\]\nIts least eigenvalue is at least\n\\[\n\\tfrac34b^{1/2}(|x|^2+b^2)^{-1/4}.\n\\]\nIt is therefore at least \\(cb^{1/2}\\) on the whole ball and a positive \\(C_3\\)-dependent constant on \\(|x|\\le C_3b\\).</p><p>Define\n\\[\n\\delta_p=\\frac{\\psi(f_p+u_p)+\\psi(f_p-u_p)}2-\\psi(f_p)\\ge0,\n\\]\n\\[\nn_p=\\frac{\\psi(f_p+u_p)-\\psi(f_p-u_p)}2.\n\\]\nTaylor's formula gives\n\\[\n\\delta_p\\ge cb^{1/2}|u_p|^2,\n\\qquad\n\\delta_p\\ge c(C_3)\\eta_p|u_p|^2,\n\\qquad\nn_p^2\\le Cb|u_p|^2\\le Cb^{1/2}\\delta_p.\n\\]\nFor the masked estimate the whole segment between child moments lies in the \\(C_3b\\)-ball.</p><p>Actual pre-child potential is\n\\[\n\\psi(f_p)+\\delta_p+r_{j+1}n_p.\n\\]\nA removal of mass \\(\\xi\\) changes it by at most \\(Cb^{1/2}\\xi\\). Telescoping gives, for every prefix,\n\\[\n\\sum_p\\delta_p\n\\le Cb^{1/2}(1+K_e)-\\sum_pr_{j+1}n_p.\n\\]\nApply Lemma 7.1 with \\(\\theta=\\gamma b^{-1/2}\\), choosing \\(\\gamma\\) small enough to absorb half the drift. Then\n\\[\n\\sum_p\\eta_p|u_p|^2\n\\le Cb^{1/2}(1+K_e+t)\n\\]\nsimultaneously for prefixes.</p><p>The delayed coefficient \\(\\eta_pu_p\\epsilon_j\\) is known before \\(r_{j+2}\\). Apply Lemma 7.1 to its components with \\(\\theta=b^{-1/4}\\). The quadratic bound gives the desired prefix estimate. Subtract two prefixes for any interval, retaining the fixed mask.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Energy drift and innovations — original Lemma 8.3.** Fix a main representative \\(s\\) on a live plateau.", "statement": "<p>**Energy drift and innovations — original Lemma 8.3.**</p><p>Fix a main representative \\(s\\) on a live plateau. At a post state write\n\\[\nM=\\mathbb E_p[aK_A(z-Y)]/\\alpha,\n\\qquad\nf=\\mathbb E_p[a\\chi_s(Y)K_A(z-Y)]/\\alpha=Mv.\n\\]\nFor prospective children write \\(f_\\pm=M_\\pm v_\\pm\\), with \\(|v|,|v_\\pm|\\le1\\), assigning zero at zero mass. Put\n\\[\nD=(M_+-M_-)/2,\\quad a=(f_+-f_-)/2,\n\\]\n\\[\nG=\\int M|v|^2,\\quad\nJ=\\int D^2/M,\\quad\nG_\\pm=\\int M_\\pm|v_\\pm|^2,\n\\]\n\\[\ng_z=\\tfrac12\\bigl(M_+|v_+-v|^2+M_-|v_--v|^2\\bigr),\n\\quad\ng=\\int g_z,\\quad\nU_p=(G_+-G_-)/2.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\ng_z=\\frac{M_+|v_+|^2+M_-|v_-|^2}2-M|v|^2\\ge0,\n\\qquad\n|a-vD|^2\\le Mg_z,\n\\]\n\\[\nG_{\\mathrm{actual\\ pre}}=G+g+r_{j+1}U_p,\n\\qquad\n|U_p|^2\\le C\\max(G_+,G_-)(J+g),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left(\\int|a|\\right)^2\\le C(GJ+g).\n\\]\nA removal of normalized mass \\(\\xi\\) changes \\(G\\) by at most \\(3\\xi\\). Forward from any live post state, conditionally under spatial uniform sampling,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sum g\\le C,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E\\sum J\\le Cd,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E\\sum\\xi\\le2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Child averaging gives\n\\[\nM_+(v_+-v)=a-vD,\\qquad M_-(v_--v)=-(a-vD).\n\\]\nExpanding around \\(v\\) gives the energy identity. For positive child masses,\n\\[\ng_z=\\frac{M|a-vD|^2}{M^2-D^2}.\n\\]\nIf a child mass is zero, \\(a-vD=0\\). Both cases give the innovation bound.</p><p>Write \\(e=a-vD\\). Expanding the child-energy difference gives\n\\[\nU_p=\\int D|v|^2+2\\operatorname{Re}\\int\\overline ve+R,\n\\qquad |R|\\le g.\n\\]\nThe first two integrals are bounded by \\(\\sqrt{GJ}\\) and \\(\\sqrt{Gg}\\). Since\n\\[\nG+g=(G_++G_-)/2\\le\\max(G_+,G_-),\n\\]\nthe squared bound follows. Also\n\\[\n\\int|a|\n\\le\\sqrt{GJ}+\\sqrt{\\left(\\int M\\right)g}\n\\le\\sqrt{GJ}+\\sqrt{2g}.\n\\]</p><p>For removals, use\n\\[\nG=\\sup_{|\\gamma|\\le1}\\int\n(2\\operatorname{Re}(\\gamma f)-|\\gamma|^2M).\n\\]\nRemoving \\(k_M\\ge0\\) and \\(k_f\\) with \\(|k_f|\\le k_M\\) changes every test by at most \\(3\\int k_M=3\\xi\\).</p><p>Finally telescope the energy identity along the stopped future plateau path. Martingale terms average to zero, terminal pre energy is at most four, and expected removals at most two. This gives the \\(g\\)-bound. The other bounds follow from Lemma 6.1 and disjoint removals.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Drift on a run — original Lemma 8.4.** Use the main-level notation of Proposition 7.", "statement": "<p>**Drift on a run — original Lemma 8.4.**</p><p>Use the main-level notation of Proposition 7. A jump is outer-valid if exceptional or\n\\[\n\\max(G_+,G_-)\\le2C_4b^2.\n\\]\nStart predictably at an outer-valid jump and stop before the first outer-invalid jump or at plateau end. For every \\(t\\ge0\\), outside conditional probability \\(e^{-t}\\), every prefix \\(\\mathcal P\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P}g_p\n\\le\nC\\left(1+\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P}\\xi_p+\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P}e'_p\\right)\n+Cb\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P}J_p+Cbt.\n\\]\nRemoval sums may be enlarged to the whole plateau.</p>", "proof": "<p>Telescope Lemma 7.3's exact energy identity. Initial and terminal energies are bounded by four, and removals cost at most \\(3\\xi\\). On exceptional jumps pay the noise directly:\n\\[\n|U_p|\\le4\\le4e'_p.\n\\]\nOn remaining outer-valid jumps,\n\\[\n|U_p|^2\\le Cb^2(J_p+g_p).\n\\]\nApply Lemma 7.1 to minus this noise with \\(\\theta=\\gamma/b\\). Its quadratic term is at most\n\\[\nC\\gamma b\\sum_p(J_p+g_p).\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\gamma\\) small enough to absorb the \\(g\\)-term, using \\(b\\le1\\). The tail term is \\(Cbt\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Delayed norm estimate on a run — original Lemma 8.5.** Under Lemma 7.4's hypotheses, put \\(\\lambda=b^{1/2}\\) and \\( W_{\\mathcal P}(z) = \\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P}a_p(z)\\epsilon_{j(p)}r_{j(p)+2}. \\) With conditional failure …", "statement": "<p>**Delayed norm estimate on a run — original Lemma 8.5.**</p><p>Under Lemma 7.4's hypotheses, put \\(\\lambda=b^{1/2}\\) and\n\\[\nW_{\\mathcal P}(z)\n=\n\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P}a_p(z)\\epsilon_{j(p)}r_{j(p)+2}.\n\\]\nWith conditional failure probability at most \\(Ce^{-t}\\), every run prefix satisfies\n\\[\n\\int|W_{\\mathcal P}|\n\\le\nC\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P}J_p\n+C\\lambda^{-1}\n\\left(1+\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P}\\xi_p+\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P}e'_p\\right)\n+C\\lambda t.\n\\]\nThe Fisher coefficient is absolute, independent of \\(b\\) and representative counts.</p>", "proof": "<p>Separate contributing depths into their two parities. For one parity, group successive jumps into blocks \\(j,j+1\\), with \\(j\\) contributing. Use complete blocks in the norm telescope. At a block start let \\(W^{(0)}\\) be the accumulated parity transform, and put\n\\[\nX_0=(W^{(0)},f_0,\\lambda M_0),\n\\qquad\n\\Phi=|X_0|.\n\\]\nComplex coordinates are viewed as real pairs. After \\(r=r_{j+1}\\), let \\((\\overline M,\\overline f)\\) be the pre-child and \\((M_1,f_1)\\) its post state after removals:\n\\[\n\\overline M=M_0+rD_0,\\quad \\overline f=f_0+ra_0,\n\\]\n\\[\nM_1=\\overline M-k_M,\\quad f_1=\\overline f-k_f,\n\\quad k_M\\ge0,\\quad |k_f|\\le k_M.\n\\]\nAt the second pre-child, with \\(r'=r_{j+2}\\), the change is\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\bigl(\na_0\\epsilon_jr',\n\\ ra_0-k_f+r'a_1,\n\\ \\lambda(rD_0-k_M+r'D_1)\n\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>Pointwise put\n\\[\nq=(|v_0|^2+\\lambda^2)^{1/2},\n\\qquad H=M_0q\\le\\Phi,\n\\qquad j_i=D_i^2/M_i,\n\\qquad e_i=a_i-v_iD_i.\n\\]\nFor \\(M_0&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nq\\ge\\lambda,\\qquad |v_0|^2/q\\le1,\n\\]\n\\[\n0\\le M_1\\le\\overline M\\le2M_0,\\quad\n0\\le k_M\\le2M_0,\\quad\n|D_i|\\le M_i,\\quad |e_i|^2\\le M_ig_{i,z}.\n\\]\nThe child post mean satisfies\n\\[\nM_1|v_1-v_0|^2\\le4g_{0,z}+8k_M.\n\\]\nIndeed the actual pre-child mean \\(\\overline v\\) has\n\\(\\overline M|\\overline v-v_0|^2\\le2g_{0,z}\\). If \\(M_1\\le\\overline M/2\\), use \\(M_1\\le k_M\\) and \\(|v_1-\\overline v|\\le2\\). Otherwise use\n\\[\nM_1(v_1-\\overline v)=k_M\\overline v-k_f\n\\]\nand \\(k_M&lt;M_1\\). The squared triangle inequality gives the claim, including \\(M_1=0\\).</p><p>Splitting \\(a_0=v_0D_0+e_0\\) gives\n\\[\n|a_0|^2/H\\le2j_0+2g_{0,z}/\\lambda.\n\\]\nSplitting\n\\[\na_1=v_0D_1+(v_1-v_0)D_1+e_1\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n|a_1|^2/H\n\\le6j_1+(24g_{0,z}+6g_{1,z}+48k_M)/\\lambda.\n\\]\nThe remaining coordinates satisfy\n\\[\n\\lambda^2D_0^2/H\\le j_0,\\quad\n\\lambda^2D_1^2/H\\le2j_1,\\quad\n(k_M^2+|k_f|^2)/H\\le4k_M/\\lambda.\n\\]\nNo lower bound on \\(M_1/M_0\\) is used. If \\(M_0=0\\), positivity makes all masses, differences, and removals in \\(\\Delta\\) zero.</p><p>For \\(\\Phi&gt;0\\), concavity of the square root gives\n\\[\n|X_0+\\Delta|\n\\le\\Phi+\\langle X_0/\\Phi,\\Delta\\rangle+\\frac{|\\Delta|^2}{2\\Phi}.\n\\]\nThe pointwise estimates imply\n\\[\n\\int\\frac{|\\Delta|^2}{2\\Phi}\n\\le C(J_0+J_1)+C\\lambda^{-1}(g_0+g_1+\\xi_{j+1}).\n\\]\nThe absolute Fisher coefficient results from retaining \\(q\\), rather than replacing it by \\(\\lambda\\), in the \\(v_0D_i\\) terms.</p><p>Let \\(\\Gamma_0=X_0/\\Phi\\), zero where \\(\\Phi=0\\). The integrated linear term is\n\\[\nrL_0+r'L_1+L_{\\mathrm{kill}},\n\\qquad |L_{\\mathrm{kill}}|\\le2\\xi_{j+1},\n\\]\nwhere \\(L_0\\) pairs \\(\\Gamma_0\\) with \\((0,a_0,\\lambda D_0)\\), and \\(L_1\\) pairs it with \\((a_0\\epsilon_j,a_1,\\lambda D_1)\\). Lemma 7.3 and\n\\[\n\\left(\\int|D_i|\\right)^2\\le\\left(\\int M_i\\right)J_i\\le2J_i\n\\]\ngive\n\\[\n|L_0|^2+|L_1|^2\n\\le C\\sum_{i=0,1}\\bigl((\\lambda^2+G_i)J_i+g_i\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>Timing is predictable: \\(W^{(0)}\\) is known at depth \\(j\\), so \\(L_0\\) is known before \\(r\\); after \\(r\\), the second post state, removals, and \\(\\epsilon_j\\) are known, so \\(L_1\\) is known before \\(r'\\). Include the first coefficient whenever its first jump is in the run, and the second only when its second jump is also in the run. Complete-block sums are prefixes of this predictable sign scheme; no coefficient is retrospectively conditioned on a later validity decision.</p><p>On nonexceptional states \\(G_i\\le2C_4b^2\\); on exceptional states \\(G_i,J_i\\le2\\) and \\(e'_i\\ge1\\). Thus\n\\[\nG_iJ_i\\le Cb^2J_i+Ce'_i.\n\\]\nApply Lemma 7.1 with parameter \\(1/\\lambda\\). Its quadratic term is bounded by\n\\[\nC\\sum J_p+C\\lambda^{-1}\\sum(g_p+e'_p),\n\\]\nand tail by \\(\\lambda t\\).</p><p>Initially \\(W^{(0)}=0\\) and \\(\\int\\Phi\\le C\\). Passing from a pre state to a post state changes integrated norm by at most \\(2\\xi\\). Telescoping complete blocks therefore gives\n\\[\nC\\sum J_p+\nC\\lambda^{-1}\\left(1+\\sum\\xi_p+\\sum e'_p+\\sum g_p\\right)\n+C\\lambda t.\n\\]\nAt most one parity output is unmatched at a prefix end; pay it directly by \\(\\int|a_p|\\le2\\), even if its delayed sign occurs after stopping. Start the other parity conditionally at its first jump. Each prefix jump is used at most twice.</p><p>Finally Lemma 7.4 bounds the \\(g\\)-sum. Since \\(b/\\lambda=\\lambda\\le1\\), its Fisher term has an absolute coefficient and its tail is \\(C\\lambda t\\). Adding the parity bounds proves the result.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Arbitrary inner-valid intervals — original Proposition 8.6.** Fix a live plateau, a main level \\(b\\), and a representative \\(s\\) at its predictable birth.", "statement": "<p>**Arbitrary inner-valid intervals — original Proposition 8.6.**</p><p>Fix a live plateau, a main level \\(b\\), and a representative \\(s\\) at its predictable birth. There is a family of predictable estimate origins, consisting of outer-valid run starts and Fisher checkpoints, with conditional expected size at most\n\\[\nP(d,b_0^{-1})\n\\]\nfor an absolute fixed polynomial \\(P\\).</p><p>For every \\(t\\ge0\\), outside conditional probability\n\\[\nCP(d,b_0^{-1})e^{-t},\n\\]\nevery contiguous inner-valid interval \\(\\mathcal I\\) after birth satisfies\n\\[\n\\int\\left|\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal I}\na_p(z)\\epsilon_{j(p)}r_{j(p)+2}\\right|\\,dz\n\\le\nC\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal I}J_p\n+Cb^{-1/2}(1+K_e+E_{e,b})\n+Cb^{1/2}t.\n\\]\nRepresentatives remain eligible after their snapshot is replaced.</p>", "proof": "<p>Start a run at the first inner-valid opportunity after birth. Follow outer-valid jumps until just before the first outer-invalid jump. Restart at the next inner-valid opportunity. The prospective energies and exception indicator make these rules predictable, and every contiguous inner-valid interval lies in one run.</p><p>Exceptional starts are charged to exceptional jumps, whose forward expected count is \\(d\\,\\operatorname{poly}(h)\\) by Lemma 6.3. At a nonexceptional start,\n\\[\nG_{\\mathrm{start}}\\le C_4b^2.\n\\]\nIf the run breaks, include its first invalid jump in a comparison interval. Its actual pre-child energy exceeds \\(2C_4b^2\\) with conditional probability at least one half; mark that break. Different start-to-break comparisons are disjoint, including invalid jumps.</p><p>For one such comparison \\(\\mathcal Q\\), put\n\\[\nM_{\\mathcal Q}=\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal Q}r_{j(p)+1}U_p.\n\\]\nA marked break implies\n\\[\nC_4b^2\\le\\sum_{\\mathcal Q}g_p+3\\sum_{\\mathcal Q}\\xi_p+|M_{\\mathcal Q}|,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n\\mathbf1_{\\{\\mathcal Q\\text{ marked}\\}}\n\\le\nCb^{-2}\\sum_{\\mathcal Q}(g_p+\\xi_p)\n+Cb^{-4}|M_{\\mathcal Q}|^2.\n\\]\nInclude comparisons and any unfinished terminal interval before conditioning on marks. Predictable stopped orthogonality gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{\\mathcal Q}|M_{\\mathcal Q}|^2\n=\n\\mathbb E\\sum_{p\\in\\cup\\mathcal Q}U_p^2\n\\le C\\mathbb E\\sum_p(J_p+g_p).\n\\]\nLemma 7.3 therefore bounds nonexceptional-start broken runs by\n\\[\nCb^{-2}+Cdb^{-4}.\n\\]\nAt most one final run does not break. Adding exceptional starts gives the required polynomial start count.</p><p>Within each run, start another estimate at the next jump after accumulated \\(J\\) since the preceding origin reaches one. The rule is predictable; each such origin's estimate continues to the run's end. The number of added origins is at most total \\(J\\), with conditional expectation \\(Cd\\). Since \\(J_p\\le2\\), checkpoint overshoot is at most two.</p><p>Apply Lemma 7.5 conditionally at every origin. Summing conditional failure probabilities weights each origin by its reaching probability, giving the stated failure bound.</p><p>For a desired interval \\(\\mathcal I\\), choose the latest origin \\(q\\) in its run at or before its first jump. The Fisher sum from \\(q\\) to that first jump is at most three. Subtract two prefix estimates from \\(q\\). Their Fisher sums add to\n\\[\n\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal I}J_p\n+2\\sum_{q\\le p&lt;\\min\\mathcal I}J_p\n\\le\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal I}J_p+6.\n\\]\nRemoval and exception sums are at most twice their plateau totals. The constant is absorbed by \\(b^{-1/2}(1+K_e+E_{e,b})\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Modulated delayed Haar estimate — original Proposition 6.1.** There are absolute \\(C,d_0\\) such that the following holds.", "statement": "<p>**Modulated delayed Haar estimate — original Proposition 6.1.**</p><p>There are absolute \\(C,d_0\\) such that the following holds. Let \\(d\\ge d_0\\), \\(h=\\log\\log d\\), and let \\(I\\subset\\mathbb R\\) be half-open with \\(1/2\\le|I|\\le1\\). Let\n\\[\n\\rho:I\\to[0,\\infty),\\qquad\n\\rho\\le e^d\\ \\text{a.e.},\\qquad\nm_{\\mathrm{top}}=\\frac1{|I|}\\int_I\\rho\\le2.\n\\]\nGive \\(I\\) normalized uniform probability \\(U\\). Let \\(\\mathcal F_j\\) be its depth-\\(j\\) dyadic sigma field, \\(p_j(x)\\) its depth-\\(j\\) interval, and \\(r_{j+1}\\) the child sign.</p><p>Let \\(J\\ge1\\), \\(Q\\ge0\\) be integers. For \\(0\\le j&lt;J\\), let \\(\\epsilon_j\\) be \\(\\mathcal F_{j+1}\\)-measurable, \\(|\\epsilon_j|\\le1\\), and common to all modulations. Retain bits through depth \\(J+1\\). Define\n\\[\n\\tau(x)=\n\\min\\bigl(\\{0\\le j&lt;J:\\mathbb E_{p_j(x)}\\rho&gt;e^{4h}\\}\\cup\\{J\\}\\bigr)\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nH^u_{I,J}\\rho(x)\n=\n\\sum_{0\\le j&lt;\\tau(x)}\n\\mathbb E_{p_j(x)}[\\rho(Y)\\chi_u(Y)r_{j+1}(Y)]\n\\epsilon_j(x)r_{j+2}(x).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\int_I\\max_{\\substack{u\\in\\mathbb Z\\\\|u|\\le Q}}\n|H^u_{I,J}\\rho(x)|\\,\\frac{dx}{|I|}\n\\le Cd.\n\\]\nThe constants are independent of \\(I,\\rho,J,Q\\), and the multipliers. The same bound holds with \\(\\sup_{u\\in\\mathbb Z}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the assembly in original Section 9.</p><p>The zero input is immediate. Apply Proposition 5. For a plateau \\(e\\), root \\(p_e\\), normalization \\(\\alpha_e\\), and post density \\(a_p\\), define\n\\[\nc_p^u=\\alpha_e^{-1}\\mathbb E_p[a_p(Y)\\chi_u(Y)r_{j(p)+1}(Y)],\n\\]\n\\[\nT_e^u(x)=\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P_e(x)}\nc_p^u\\epsilon_{j(p)}(x)r_{j(p)+2}(x),\n\\qquad\nw_e=U(p_e)\\alpha_e.\n\\]\nExact splitting gives\n\\[\nH^u_{I,J}\\rho(x)=\\sum_{e:x\\in p_e}\\alpha_eT_e^u(x),\n\\]\nand therefore\n\\[\n\\int\\max_{|u|\\le Q}|H^u_{I,J}\\rho|\\,dU\n\\le\\sum_ew_e\\mathbb E_{U_e}\\sup_u|T_e^u|.\n\\]\nWe have \\(|c_p^u|\\le2\\) and \\(\\mathbb E_{U_e}K_e\\le1\\).</p><p>Use main levels\n\\[\n\\mathcal B_m=\\{2^{-j}:b_0\\le2^{-j}\\le1\\}\n\\]\non live plateaus. Use scalar levels\n\\[\n\\mathcal B_s(B)=\\{B2^{-j}:j\\ge0\\},\n\\]\nwith \\(B=b_0\\) live and \\(B=1\\) static. At \\(b_0\\), main and scalar constructions are distinct. Proposition 7 supplies parameters \\(t_b^m,t_b^s,H_b^m,H_b^s\\).</p><p>For scalar edge accounting, at each piece choose its largest bad scalar level \\(\\bar b(p)\\), if any, and put\n\\[\nE_p^s=e_{p,\\bar b(p)};\n\\]\notherwise put \\(E_p^s=0\\). This is predictable and independent of \\(u\\). The edge regions are nested in \\(b\\), so\n\\[\nb\\le\\bar b(p)\\Longrightarrow e_{p,b}\\le E_p^s.\n\\]\nWrite\n\\[\nE_{p,b}^m=e_{p,b}\\mathbf1_{\\{p\\text{ bad at main level }b\\}}.\n\\]</p><p>**Main amplitudes and decoding.**\nFix \\(u\\) on a live plateau path. From its first main activation onward, retain the largest main level activated so far. This is nondecreasing, so each level occurs on at most one interval. Fix the decomposition at its first activation:\n\\[\nu=s+n+a',\n\\qquad\n\\Delta_A(n)\\le r_{\\mathrm{sep}},\n\\qquad\n|a'||p_{\\mathrm{act}}|\\le C\\sigma.\n\\]\nFor every prospective child \\(q\\) below activation, conditional Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\|f_q^u-\\chi_n(\\cdot)\\chi_{a'}(x)f_q^s\\|_{L^2(M_q^{-1}dz)}\n\\le C\\Delta_A(n)+C|a'||p|,\n\\]\nbecause the channel part has squared bound\n\\[\n\\alpha_e^{-1}\\mathbb E_q\\left[\na_p(Y)\\int|\\chi_n(Y)-\\chi_n(z)|^2K_A(z-Y)\\,dz\n\\right]\n=\n(\\mathbb E_qa_p/\\alpha_e)\\Delta_A(n)^2,\n\\]\nand the spatial part uses the child mass bound. Hence\n\\[\n|\\sqrt{G_q^u}-\\sqrt{G_q^s}|\n\\le C(r_{\\mathrm{sep}}+\\sigma)\\ll b_0.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(b&lt;1\\), level \\(2b\\) has not activated on its assigned interval. Remove the intersection with the one preactivation block for non-bad \\(2b\\)-high jumps and pay \\(CH_{2b}^m\\) directly. At most two contiguous intervals remain. On each, every nonexceptional jump is not \\(2b\\)-high for \\(u\\), so it is inner-valid for \\(s,b\\). For \\(b=1\\), all jumps are inner-valid and no block is removed.</p><p>Let\n\\[\na_p^s(z)=\n\\alpha_e^{-1}\\mathbb E_p[\na_p(Y)\\chi_s(Y)r_{j+1}(Y)K_A(z-Y)],\n\\]\n\\[\nc_A(n)=\\int\\chi_nK_A\n=1-\\tfrac12\\Delta_A(n)^2\\ge\\tfrac12.\n\\]\nThe exact decoding identity is\n\\[\n\\int\\chi_n(z)a_p^s(z)\\,dz=c_A(n)c_p^{s+n}.\n\\]\nOn any subset of descendants of activation,\n\\[\n\\sum_p|c_p^u-\\chi_{a'}(x)c_p^{s+n}|\n\\le C|a'|\\sum_p|p|\n\\le C\\sigma.\n\\]\nThus for any retained subinterval \\(\\mathcal L\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal L}c_p^u\\epsilon_jr_{j+2}\\right|\n\\le\n2\\int\\left|\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal L}a_p^s(z)\\epsilon_jr_{j+2}\\right|\\,dz\n+C\\sigma.\n\\]</p><p>Apply Proposition 7. Fisher charges occur only on the retained subintervals, with bounded multiplicity across levels. Enlarge kill and exceptional sums to the whole plateau. Since\n\\[\ne'_p=Cb_0^{-2}E_{p,2b}^m,\n\\qquad\nb^{-1/2}b_0^{-2}\\le b_0^{-3},\n\\]\nthe main contribution is bounded by\n\\[\nC\\left\\{\n\\sum_pJ_p\n+b_0^{-3}\\sum_p\\sum_{b\\in\\mathcal B_m}E_{p,b}^m\n+\n\\sum_{b\\in\\mathcal B_m}\n\\bigl[b^{-1/2}(1+K_e)+b^{1/2}t_b^m+H_b^m\\bigr]\n\\right\\}.\n\\]</p><p>**Scalar prefix and static paths.**\nFor a live plateau use the prefix before its first main activation; for a static plateau use the whole path. Retain the largest scalar level activated so far, or zero. Each positive level occupies at most one contiguous interval.</p><p>At level \\(b\\), activation gives \\(u=s+a'\\) with\n\\[\n|a'||p_{\\mathrm{act}}|\\le Cb^4.\n\\]\nApply the scalar estimate to its fixed mask\n\\[\n\\max(|f_{p,+}^s|,|f_{p,-}^s|)\\le C_3b.\n\\]\nFreezing the spatial discrepancy changes the masked sum by at most \\(Cb^4\\), by the geometric interval-length sum. All masked scalar contributions cost\n\\[\nC\\sum_{b\\in\\mathcal B_s(B)}\nb^{1/4}(1+K_e+t_b^s).\n\\]</p><p>Pay remaining jumps directly. Put\n\\[\nA'_p=\\max(|f_{p,+}^u|,|f_{p,-}^u|)\\le4.\n\\]\nTheir coefficient cost is at most \\(A'_p\\). If positive level \\(b\\) is retained and its mask fails, phase comparison gives\n\\[\nA'_p\\ge C_3b-Cb^4&gt;4b.\n\\]</p><p>On a live prefix, a direct jump with \\(A'_p&gt;4b_0\\) is main-high at \\(b_0\\), since\n\\[\n|f_{p,\\pm}^u|^2\n\\le\\left(\\int M_{p,\\pm}\\right)G_{p,\\pm}^u\\le4G_{p,\\pm}^u.\n\\]\nNo main level has activated. Non-bad such jumps cost their one miss block, and bad ones cost \\(Cb_0^{-2}E_{p,b_0}^m\\), already covered by the main majorant.</p><p>Otherwise \\(0&lt;A'_p\\le4B\\). Choose the largest available scalar level \\(\\widetilde b\\le A'_p\\). Then\n\\[\nA'_p/4\\le\\widetilde b\\le A'_p.\n\\]\nThis level is high and not yet activated: it is larger than any currently retained positive level. Non-bad jumps assigned to \\(\\widetilde b\\) lie in its one missed block and cost \\(C\\widetilde bH_{\\widetilde b}^s\\). For bad assigned levels,\n\\[\nA'_p\\le4\\widetilde b\\le Ce_{p,\\widetilde b}\\le CE_p^s.\n\\]\nOnly one predictable scalar edge level is charged per jump.</p><p>Consequently a frequency-independent majorant on a live plateau is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC^{-1}\\sup_u|T_e^u|\n\\le{}&amp;\n\\sum_p\\left(J_p+b_0^{-3}\\sum_{b\\in\\mathcal B_m}E_{p,b}^m+E_p^s\\right)\\\\\n&amp;+\\sum_{b\\in\\mathcal B_m}\n\\left[b^{-1/2}(1+K_e)+b^{1/2}t_b^m+H_b^m\\right]\\\\\n&amp;+\\sum_{b\\in\\mathcal B_s(b_0)}\n\\left[b^{1/4}(1+K_e+t_b^s)+bH_b^s\\right].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor static plateaus the analogous majorant consists only of the scalar edge sum and scalar series with \\(B=1\\). The constructions hold outside one plateau null set and introduce no frequency-dependent exceptional set.</p><p>**Summing levels and live weights.**\nIf \\(b_0=2^{-M}\\), then \\(M=O(1+\\log h)\\), and\n\\[\n\\sum_{b\\in\\mathcal B_m}b^{1/2}k_{b/4}\n=\nA_0\\frac dh\\sum_{j=0}^{M}\n2^{-j/2}(1+(j+2)\\log2)^2\n\\le Cd/h.\n\\]\nThe additional logarithmic tails cost \\(O(\\log(Cd))\\). Also\n\\[\n\\sum_{b\\in\\mathcal B_m}b^{-1/2}\\mathbb E(1+K_e)\n\\le Cb_0^{-1/2}\\le Ch^8,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_{b\\in\\mathcal B_m}\\mathbb EH_b^m\n\\le C(1+\\log h)(\\log(Cd)+\\log h).\n\\]\nEvery fixed polynomial in \\(h\\), times \\(1+\\log d\\), is \\(o(d/h)\\). Thus non-Fisher, non-edge main costs are \\(O(d/h)\\) per live plateau.</p><p>Geometric scalar summation gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{b\\in\\mathcal B_s(B)}\n\\mathbb E\\left[b^{1/4}(1+K_e+t_b^s)+bH_b^s\\right]\n\\le\nCB^{1/4}(d_1+1+\\log(1/B)+\\log(1/\\sigma)).\n\\]\nFor live plateaus this is \\(O(d/h^4)\\); for static plateaus it is \\(O(d_1)\\). Lemma 6.3 therefore gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{U_e}\\sup_u|T_e^u|\n\\le C(d+2+\\log^-\\alpha_e)\n\\]\nfor static plateaus.</p><p>For every nonnegative jump quantity \\(V_p\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_ew_e\\mathbb E_{U_e}\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P_e}V_p\n=\n\\sum_{\\text{jumping pieces }p}U(p)\\alpha_eV_p.\n\\]\nProposition 5 bounds the global live Fisher charge by \\(Cd\\). Lemma 6.3 bounds the selected scalar edge charge by \\(Cd\\), and the main edge charge, even after \\(b_0^{-3}\\), is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{poly}(h)(h+\\log d)=o(d).\n\\]\nThe live plateau weights sum to \\(Ch\\); multiplying their \\(O(d/h)\\) residual cost gives \\(Cd\\).</p><p>**Static families and transfers.**\nA static family entering with mean \\(m&gt;0\\) at \\(q\\) has normalizations doubling at each reset. Jumping plateau normalizations are at most \\(e^{4h}\\), so along its normalized input path\n\\[\nN_{\\mathrm{st}}\\le C(1+h+\\log^-m),\n\\qquad\nd+2+\\log^-\\alpha_e\\le d+2+\\log^-m.\n\\]\nChanging from input reaching probabilities to starting weights bounds its entire cost by\n\\[\nCU(q)m(1+h+\\log^-m)(d+2+\\log^-m).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(0&lt;m_{\\mathrm{top}}&lt;1/h\\), put \\(\\ell=\\log(1/m_{\\mathrm{top}})\\). Its initial static family costs\n\\[\nCm_{\\mathrm{top}}(1+h+\\ell)(d+2+\\ell)\\le Cd,\n\\]\nusing \\(m_{\\mathrm{top}}h\\le1\\), boundedness of \\(t\\log(1/t)\\) and \\(t\\log^2(1/t)\\), and \\(h\\le d\\).</p><p>For an initially live top, each true input transfers at most once. Let \\(A_{\\mathrm{tr}}\\) be transfer, and use Proposition 5:\n\\[\nP(A_{\\mathrm{tr}})\\le Ch^{-10},\n\\qquad\n\\|X_*\\|_2\\le Ch.\n\\]\nAt transfer, \\(\\log^-m\\le X_*\\). Summing static-family costs gives at most\n\\[\nCm_{\\mathrm{top}}\n\\mathbb E[\n\\mathbf1_{A_{\\mathrm{tr}}}\n(1+h+X_*)(d+2+X_*)].\n\\]\nIf \\(\\delta=P(A_{\\mathrm{tr}})\\), Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[\\mathbf1_{A_{\\mathrm{tr}}}X_*]\n\\le\\sqrt\\delta\\,\\|X_*\\|_2\\le Ch^{-4},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E[\\mathbf1_{A_{\\mathrm{tr}}}X_*^2]\\le Ch^2.\n\\]\nThus the expectation is at most\n\\[\nCdh^{-9}+C(d+h)h^{-4}+Ch^2\\le Cd.\n\\]\nSince \\(m_{\\mathrm{top}}\\le2\\), all static descendants cost \\(Cd\\).</p><p>These estimates bound the initial weighted sum by \\(Cd\\), uniformly in \\(Q\\). Monotone convergence as \\(Q\\to\\infty\\) gives the full modulation supremum.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Truncated integral estimate — original Theorem 10.1.** Use unit-period coordinates and normalized measure \\(dx\\).", "statement": "<p>**Truncated integral estimate — original Theorem 10.1.**</p><p>Use unit-period coordinates and normalized measure \\(dx\\). For nonnegative periodic \\(\\rho\\), define the uncentered interval maximal function\n\\[\nM\\rho(x)=\n\\sup_{\\substack{I\\ni x\\\\0&lt;|I|\\le1}}\n\\frac1{|I|}\\int_I\\rho,\n\\]\nwith intervals on the periodic extension.</p><p>There are absolute \\(C,d_*\\) such that, whenever\n\\[\nd\\ge d_*,\n\\qquad\n\\rho\\ge0,\n\\qquad\n\\int_0^1\\rho=1,\n\\qquad\n\\rho\\le e^d\\ \\text{a.e.},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nh=\\log\\log d,\\qquad G_\\rho=\\{M\\rho\\le e^{4h}\\},\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n|G_\\rho^c|\\le C(\\log d)^{-4},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\int_{G_\\rho}\\sup_{N\\ge0}|S_N\\rho(x)|\\,dx\\le Cd.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\widehat\\rho(k)=\\int_0^1\\rho(x)e^{-2\\pi ikx}\\,dx\\), and \\(S_N\\) is the ordinary symmetric Fourier projection.</p>", "proof": "<p>The interval covering argument gives\n\\[\n|\\{M\\rho&gt;\\lambda\\}|\n\\le\\frac C\\lambda\\int_0^1\\rho.\n\\]\nFor a compact subset of the level set, take finitely many witnessing intervals and a disjoint subfamily whose fixed enlargements cover the subset. Their total length is at most \\(\\lambda^{-1}\\int\\rho\\), up to absolute periodic overlap. Inner regularity proves the bound. Therefore\n\\[\n|G_\\rho^c|\\le Ce^{-4h}=C(\\log d)^{-4}.\n\\]</p><p>Lemmas 9.1 and 9.2 give\n\\[\n\\int_{G_\\rho}\\sup_n|H(\\rho\\chi_n)|\\le Cd.\n\\]\nSince \\(|\\widehat\\rho(k)|\\le1\\), the projection identities yield\n\\[\n\\sup_{N\\ge0}|S_N\\rho|\n\\le\\rho+1+\\sup_n|H(\\rho\\chi_n)|.\n\\]\nIntegrating over \\(G_\\rho\\) proves the truncated integral estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Averaged delayed Haar kernels — original Section 10.1, through equation (10.7).** Under Proposition 9's assumptions, extend by zero outside \\([0,1)\\) \\( h_0=\\mathbf1_{[0,1/2)}-\\mathbf1_{[1/2,1)}, \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Averaged delayed Haar kernels — original Section 10.1, through equation (10.7).**</p><p>Under Proposition 9's assumptions, extend by zero outside \\([0,1)\\)\n\\[\nh_0=\\mathbf1_{[0,1/2)}-\\mathbf1_{[1/2,1)},\n\\]\n\\[\ng_0=\n\\mathbf1_{[0,1/4)}\n-\\mathbf1_{[1/4,1/2)}\n-\\mathbf1_{[1/2,3/4)}\n+\\mathbf1_{[3/4,1)}.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\n\\kappa(u)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}g_0(v)h_0(v-u)\\,dv,\n\\qquad\nK_J(t)=\\int_{2^{-J}}^1\\kappa(t/r)\\,\\frac{dr}{r^2}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\int_{G_\\rho}\\sup_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}\n|K_J*(\\rho\\chi_n)(x)|\\,dx\\le Cd.\n\\]\nThe function \\(\\kappa\\) is odd, Lipschitz, supported in \\([-1,1]\\), and\n\\[\nc:=\\int_0^1\\kappa(u)\\,du=-1/8.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\delta=2^{-J}\\),\n\\[\nH_\\delta F(x)\n=\n\\int_{\\delta&lt;|t|\\le1/2}F(x-t)\\cot(\\pi t)\\,dt\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n\\int_{G_\\rho}\\sup_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}|H_\\delta(\\rho\\chi_n)(x)|\\,dx\\le Cd.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For a dyadic interval \\(I=[a,a+r)\\), use scaled profiles \\(h_I,g_I\\). The summand\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac1{|I|}\\int_I\\rho(y)\\chi_n(y)h_I(y)\\,dy\\right)g_I(x)\n\\]\nis admissible in Proposition 8 by choosing \\(\\epsilon_j=r_{j+1}\\), so \\(g_I=r_{j+1}r_{j+2}\\).</p><p>Tile the line by tops of length \\(s\\in[1/2,1]\\), translate by \\(a\\in[0,s)\\), and use depths \\(0,\\ldots,J-1\\). Call the shift \\(S_{a,s,J}\\). Only a bounded number of tops meet \\([0,1)\\), each with input average at most two. At \\(x\\in G_\\rho\\), every interval containing \\(x\\) has mean at most \\(e^{4h}\\), so stopping is inactive. Proposition 8 gives\n\\[\n\\int_{G_\\rho}\\sup_n|S_{a,s,J}(\\rho\\chi_n)(x)|\\,dx\\le Cd.\n\\]</p><p>For scale \\(r=s2^{-j}\\), uniform translation modulo \\(s\\) is uniform modulo \\(r\\). Fubini and the change from interval endpoint to output position show that the averaged tiling kernel is \\(r^{-1}\\kappa(t/r)\\). Integrating in \\(ds/s\\) gives\n\\[\n\\int_{1/2}^1\\int_0^sS_{a,s,J}F(x)\\,\\frac{da}{s}\\frac{ds}{s}\n=K_J*F(x),\n\\]\nbecause the scale intervals \\([2^{-j-1},2^{-j}]\\) partition \\([2^{-J},1]\\). The triangle inequality proves the convolution bound. The scale measure has mass \\(\\log2\\), absorbed in \\(C\\). Only finite-shift outputs are averaged; no measurability of auxiliary choices in Proposition 8 is needed.</p><p>Reflection about \\(1/2\\) fixes \\(g_0\\) and reverses \\(h_0\\), so \\(\\kappa\\) is odd. Interval overlap formulas give its support and Lipschitz property. Moreover,\n\\[\nc\n=\n\\int_0^1g_0(v)\\left(\\int_0^vh_0(t)\\,dt\\right)\\,dv\n=\n\\frac1{32}-\\frac3{32}-\\frac3{32}+\\frac1{32}\n=-\\frac18.\n\\]\nThe primitive is \\(\\min(v,1-v)\\).</p><p>For \\(\\delta&lt;t\\le1\\), substitution \\(u=t/r\\) gives\n\\[\nK_J(t)=\\frac1t\\int_t^1\\kappa(u)\\,du\n=\\frac ct-\\frac1t\\int_0^t\\kappa(u)\\,du.\n\\]\nThe second term is bounded, and oddness covers negative \\(t\\). On \\(|t|\\le\\delta\\),\n\\[\n|K_J(t)|\\le C\\delta^{-1}.\n\\]\nCompare with\n\\[\n\\pi c\\cot(\\pi t)\\mathbf1_{\\{\\delta&lt;|t|\\le1/2\\}}.\n\\]\nThe difference on the central annulus is bounded since\n\\(\\pi\\cot(\\pi t)-1/t\\) is bounded. The kernel on \\(1/2&lt;|t|\\le1\\) is also bounded. These errors convolve against \\(\\rho\\chi_n\\) to \\(O(1)\\), by periodicity and mass one. The inner window costs\n\\[\nC\\delta^{-1}\\int_{|t|\\le\\delta}\\rho(x-t)\\,dt\n\\le CM\\rho(x)\\le Ce^{4h}\n\\]\non \\(G_\\rho\\). Since \\(e^{4h}=o(d)\\) and \\(c\\ne0\\), the truncated Hilbert estimate follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Periodic Hilbert multiplier and Fourier projections — original Section 10.1, equations (10.8)–(10.9) and the projection identities.** For \\(F\\in L^2(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)\\), the operators \\(H_\\delta\\) from Lemma 9.1 …", "statement": "<p>**Periodic Hilbert multiplier and Fourier projections — original Section 10.1, equations (10.8)–(10.9) and the projection identities.**</p><p>For \\(F\\in L^2(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)\\), the operators \\(H_\\delta\\) from Lemma 9.1 converge in \\(L^2\\), as \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\), to \\(H\\) with multiplier\n\\[\n\\widehat{HF}(k)=-i\\,\\operatorname{sgn}(k)\\widehat F(k)\n\\quad(k\\ne0),\\qquad \\widehat{HF}(0)=0.\n\\]\nFor bounded mass-one \\(\\rho\\) as in Proposition 9,\n\\[\n\\int_{G_\\rho}\\sup_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}|H(\\rho\\chi_n)|\\le Cd.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(P_0\\) is projection onto constants and \\(\\Pi_{\\ge0}\\) onto nonnegative Fourier frequencies, then\n\\[\n\\Pi_{\\ge0}=\\frac{I+iH+P_0}{2},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nS_N\\rho\n=\n\\chi_{-N}\\Pi_{\\ge0}(\\rho\\chi_N)\n-\n\\chi_{N+1}\\Pi_{\\ge0}(\\rho\\chi_{-(N+1)}).\n\\]\nThese identities hold almost everywhere simultaneously over the indicated integer indices.</p>", "proof": "<p>Oddness reduces the multiplier of \\(H_\\delta\\) to a sine integral. The multipliers are uniformly bounded: write\n\\[\n\\cot(\\pi t)=1/(\\pi t)+O(1),\n\\]\nsplit the sine integral at inverse frequency, and integrate by parts beyond that point.</p><p>For a positive integer \\(k\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\sin(2\\pi kt)\\cot(\\pi t)\\,dt=1.\n\\]\nIndeed, with \\(x=\\pi t\\),\n\\[\n\\sin(2kx)=2\\sin x\\sum_{a=1}^k\\cos((2a-1)x).\n\\]\nAfter multiplication by \\(\\cot x\\), only the \\(a=1\\) term contributes a constant in\n\\(2\\cos x\\sum_a\\cos((2a-1)x)\\); all nonconstant even-frequency cosines integrate to zero over \\([-\\pi/2,\\pi/2]\\). Negative frequencies have opposite sign. Pointwise multiplier convergence and uniform boundedness give \\(L^2\\) convergence by Parseval.</p><p>Every \\(\\rho\\chi_n\\) is bounded, hence in \\(L^2\\). For finitely many \\(n\\), the maximum of\n\\[\n|H_\\delta(\\rho\\chi_n)-H(\\rho\\chi_n)|\n\\]\ntends to zero in \\(L^1\\), bounded by the sum of the \\(L^2\\) differences. Pass to the limit in Lemma 9.1 for a finite set, then increase to all integers by monotone convergence.</p><p>The final identities are direct \\(L^2\\) multiplier identities. Removing one countable union of null sets makes them simultaneous.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Bounded inputs — original Proposition 10.2.** Every bounded complex-valued periodic function has almost-everywhere convergence of its ordinary symmetric Fourier partial sums along the full sequence.", "statement": "<p>**Bounded inputs — original Proposition 10.2.**</p><p>Every bounded complex-valued periodic function has almost-everywhere convergence of its ordinary symmetric Fourier partial sums along the full sequence.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\|f\\|_\\infty\\le B\\); \\(B=0\\) is immediate. Let \\(P_\\ell\\) be its Fejér means. These are trigonometric polynomials with\n\\[\n\\|P_\\ell\\|_\\infty\\le B,\\qquad \\|f-P_\\ell\\|_1\\to0,\n\\]\nby the nonnegative mass-one approximate-identity property of Fejér kernels and continuity of translations in \\(L^1\\).</p><p>Split \\(f-P_\\ell\\) into positive and negative parts of its real and imaginary components. Each nonnegative component \\(q_\\ell\\) is at most \\(2B\\), with mass \\(m_\\ell\\to0\\). Zero-mass components vanish. Otherwise put\n\\[\n\\rho_\\ell=q_\\ell/m_\\ell,\\qquad\nd_\\ell=d_*+\\max\\{0,\\log(2B/m_\\ell)\\}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nd_\\ell\\to\\infty,\\qquad m_\\ell d_\\ell\\to0,\\qquad\n\\rho_\\ell\\le e^{d_\\ell}.\n\\]\nProposition 9 and Markov's inequality imply, for \\(\\eta&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\\left\\{\\sup_N|S_Nq_\\ell|&gt;\\eta\\right\\}\\right|\n\\le\nC(\\log d_\\ell)^{-4}+\\frac{Cm_\\ell d_\\ell}{\\eta}\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nApplying this to all four components proves\n\\[\n\\sup_N|S_N(f-P_\\ell)|\\to0\n\\quad\\text{in measure}.\n\\]</p><p>Set\n\\[\nA(x)=\\limsup_{N\\to\\infty}|S_Nf(x)-f(x)|.\n\\]\nBecause \\(S_NP_\\ell=P_\\ell\\) eventually,\n\\[\nA(x)\\le\n\\sup_N|S_N(f-P_\\ell)(x)|+|f(x)-P_\\ell(x)|\n\\]\nalmost everywhere. Both right-hand terms tend to zero in measure. Hence, for every \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), \\(|\\{A&gt;\\eta\\}|=0\\). Thus \\(A=0\\) almost everywhere.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 12, "cite"], [3, 5, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [4, 6, "cite"], [4, 8, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [5, 8, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [6, 10, "cite"], [7, 2, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [8, 2, "cite"], [8, 10, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [10, 2, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [11, 13, "cite"], [11, 14, "cite"], [11, 15, "cite"], [12, 15, "cite"], [12, 16, "cite"], [13, 2, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [13, 15, "cite"], [13, 16, "cite"], [14, 3, "cite"], [14, 12, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [16, 18, "cite"], [16, 20, "cite"], [16, 30, "cite"], [17, 23, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [18, 20, "cite"], [18, 21, "cite"], [18, 26, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [20, 17, "cite"], [20, 29, "cite"], [20, 30, "cite"], [21, 17, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [21, 23, "cite"], [22, 17, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [23, 30, "cite"], [24, 18, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [24, 27, "cite"], [24, 28, "cite"], [25, 23, "part"], [26, 20, "cite"], [26, 27, "cite"], [26, 28, "cite"], [26, 29, "cite"], [27, 21, "cite"], [27, 28, "cite"], [28, 22, "cite"], [28, 29, "cite"], [29, 23, "cite"], [30, 32, "cite"], [31, 0, "cite"], [31, 34, "cite"], [32, 31, "cite"], [32, 33, "cite"], [33, 31, "cite"], [34, 0, "cite"]]});
